## Book 1 — Three-Movement Architecture

### Movement 1: Kindling (Chapters 1–7)
**Entry pressure:** The protagonist's Kindling ceremony goes wrong — all three doors
open simultaneously during the fever dream. They come out of it with no single door
firmly established, a disk of unknown origin, and a classification the system cannot
generate.

**Protagonist wants:** To understand what happened. To find a category.
**What they get instead:** The system can't classify them. The classification attempt
itself draws attention.

**Key beats:**
- Ch. 1: Epigraph (institutional document about the Kindling ceremony) → Protagonist
  emerges from fever dream. Name withheld. Physical damage present (fever mark — a
  persistent scar at the door-open site). The disk appears.
- Ch. 2: Binary exemplar pair — two Kindled from a higher year demonstrate opposite
  door approaches before the protagonist's first live classification attempt.
  The system produces partial output and stalls.
- Ch. 3: Live commentary voice (an observer explains system mechanics as the
  protagonist makes their first real door-contact attempt). Results: unexpected.
- Ch. 4: First encounter with Grant. Structural dismissal. The protagonist is
  ranked below him and knows it.
- Ch. 5: Viv and Catcher — found by the protagonist or they find them. The anchor
  pair forms.
- Ch. 6: First private word from Dent. Not encouragement — a specific practical note.
  "Whatever it takes." She does not explain why she's watching.
- Ch. 7: End of Movement 1 — protagonist reaches their first real threshold. Small
  victory that cost something real. `PLANNED development beat: protagonist realizes
  the three doors are not three separate things to master. They move together.`

**Scene-break cadence target:** ~8.7 per 10k words (~950 words between breaks)
**Progression density target:** ~900 hits in this third (front-loaded)

---

### Movement 2: First Season (Chapters 8–17)
**Entry pressure:** Competitive season opens. The protagonist must compete in a
classification bracket they don't fit. The system assigns them the lowest possible
rank in a single-door category as a placeholder.

**Protagonist wants:** To prove the configuration is real. To find a teacher who
understands what they have.
**What they get instead:** Each competition reveals a new facet of the disk's behavior.
The disk is ahead of the protagonist.

**Key beats:**
- Ch. 8: First sanctioned competition. The protagonist uses a single door (the
  safest, most stable of the three). Wins. The disk's output during the win is
  unusual — the system log shows a field that doesn't belong.
- Ch. 9: Lennon is seen for the first time — from a distance, in a crowd. Catcher
  IDs him. The protagonist files it away without acting on it.
- Ch. 10: Grant returns. This time they fight in a practice setting — the protagonist
  has advanced; Grant has the tools to end it cleanly. He doesn't. He escalates.
  The escalation is the real lesson.
- Ch. 11: Dent's second private session. Longer. She demonstrates something with
  Kestrel — the protagonist reads the device's stillness and begins to understand
  the mentor-device relationship differently.
- Ch. 12: Aria Laurent has been collecting data. She knows the protagonist's disk
  output from the two competitions doesn't match any known door-configuration.
  She doesn't approach yet. She watches.
- Ch. 13–14: Two more competitions. The protagonist begins using two doors in
  combination by accident — a mid-fight discovery that the narrator reports as
  physical sensation, not as triumph. Something in the disk changes. The S-rank
  anomaly in the output becomes harder to ignore.
- Ch. 15–16: The protagonist reaches the regional bracket. They don't belong there
  by rank. They're there because the system put them in the nearest available category.
  They find a way through. `PLANNED beat: first real injury that persists.`
- Ch. 17: End of Movement 2. The protagonist has won their bracket. Lennon, competing
  at a higher level in the same facility, passes them in a hallway without speaking.
  Sees the disk. His expression changes briefly. He keeps walking.

**Progression density target:** ~471 hits in this third (receding into furniture)

---

### Movement 3: Visibility (Chapters 18–25)
**Entry pressure:** The regional win created a public record. The disk's anomalous
output is now in the official competition log. Someone who knows what the Mars disk
lineage looks like has seen it.

**Protagonist wants:** To keep advancing without being defined by what they can't explain.
**What they get instead:** The thing they carried thinking it was a disadvantage turns
out to be the reason they're interesting to dangerous people.

**Key beats:**
- Ch. 18: Dent calls the protagonist in for a third session — but this time she has
