## Core Mythology — The Soul as Power-Source

### The deepest truth of the universe (`CANON`)

Every Ember user, and every magic user, draws from the same place: a region of the
brain that conventional neuroscience could not account for — the "unused space." In
the pre-EMP era, medical researchers at a dedicated station discovered, via fMRI,
that magic practitioners and Ember practitioners were activating identical neural
structures. Not similar. Identical.

The colloquial name that survived: **the soul.**

This finding was never published before the EMP. The research station was lost.
The knowledge survived in one person: Rook.

---

## Rook — Historical Figure (Founder of the Touren Lineage)

### Who Rook was
Rook was a research subject at the medical research station — an Ember practitioner
who agreed to have his brain activity mapped during door-contact. He met his love
(a researcher at the station) during this work. He saw the fMRI images. He understood
what they meant before the researchers finished writing their paper.

When the EMP came, the station fell. Rook carried the knowledge.

**What Rook preserved:**
- The fMRI methodology (reproducible without the original equipment, once you
  know where to look)
- The unifying theory: magic and Ember share the same source — the soul,
  the unused neural space
- The practical implication: a practitioner who trains both traditions simultaneously
  is not doing two things. They are doing one thing more completely.
- The location and nature of the rifts — where the barrier between normal space
  and the soul-source thins

**What Rook founded:**
The Touren lineage. Not an institution initially — a group of practitioners he
taught directly, sharing what the research station had shown him and what he had
confirmed through his own practice.

**Rook and the disk:**
Rook carried a disk. It is not clear whether the disk was given to him at the
research station, or whether he brought it there and the researchers didn't know
what it was. What is `CANON`: Rook's disk was passed down through the Touren lineage
for 600 years. The protagonist carries it now.

**Note on the current Mentor character:**
The protagonist's Mentor (Valera Dent analog) is trained in the Touren lineage.
She knows Rook's history. She knows what the disk is. Whether she is aware the
protagonist carries Touren's disk, or whether she suspects and is waiting — is
a Book 1 story decision. `POSSIBLE`

---

## The Three Institutions

### 1. Touren Academy (`CANON` — chartered)
Built on Rook's knowledge. Formally chartered after the Touren lineage grew large
enough to need institutional structure. Teaches the unified theory of Ember and magic
as one source. Students learn door-contact as the primary language, but the
curriculum acknowledges the soul-source beneath it.

The curriculum includes rift awareness — not as combat training, but as context.
Students know what rifts are. They know the history of rift-spawn encounters.
They are not fighters (yet).

### 2. Wyck's Academy (`CANON` — chartered, separate lineage)
A different institutional tradition. Built on a parallel preservation of pre-EMP
knowledge — not Rook's lineage directly, but converging toward the same truth.
The relationship between Touren and Wyck's is competitive but not hostile.
They disagree about methodology, not about the soul-source.

The protagonist may encounter Wyck's students in competition settings.
A Wyck's student may have a characteristically different door-entry method
that produces the same kind of power by a different route.

### 3. Rook's Non-Chartered Rift-Spawn Fighters (`CANON` — informal, active)
A group that Rook himself organized in the immediate post-EMP generation and that
has never formalized. They are practitioners who specialize in rift encounters —
the places where the barrier thins and things come through.

They are not officially recognized. Their techniques are not in any curriculum.
Some of them have abilities that the classification system cannot rank because
the classification system wasn't designed around their methodology.

This group is the closest thing the world has to people who know what the
coming fight actually is. They are not warning people loudly. They are preparing.

---

## The EMP — What It Actually Was

### `CANON` — The filter is breaking, not fading

The filter is not a metaphor. It is a barrier — a literal boundary between the
civilization's physical realm and what lies beyond it: the Glory Realm. The filter
was the mechanism by which this boundary held.

The EMP broke the filter.

Whether the EMP was caused by something on the Glory Realm side (a test, a reach,
an accident), or by something within this civilization, or by the collision of
both: `RESERVED`. What is `CANON`:

- The EMP cracked the filter 600 years ago
- The "recession" of the filter over the subsequent generations is the crack
  propagating — not linear, not recoverable
- The Homura's discovery that the recession rate has accelerated in the last
  generation = the crack is widening faster now
- When the filter fully breaks: the barrier between realms fails

The classification system, the rank ladder, the training institutions — all of this
was built in the crack's shadow. Most people do not know that is what it is.

---

## The Glory Realm — What Is Beyond the Filter

### `CANON` — Glory is the entry point, not the ceiling

The civilization's Kindling rank ladder ends at Glory. This was always true. What
was not known (except to Rook, the Touren lineage, and the rift-spawn fighters):

**Glory is the floor of the next realm, not the ceiling of this one.**

The Glory Realm is populated. It has been populated for longer than anyone on this
side of the filter has been tracking. It has its own power ladder — what the first
rank above Glory is, what the ceiling is, how it is structured: `RESERVED`.

The entities and civilizations of the Glory Realm are not hostile by default. They
are what they have always been: a universe of power-filled worlds that existed on
their side of the barrier, aware that something was on the other side, unable to
reach through while the filter held.

When the filter breaks, the civilization will be integrated into the Glory Realm
as the newest participants. The question will not be "survive the invasion." The
question will be "are you strong enough to compete?"

### The three-book arc in one sentence
*Book 1:* A practitioner who doesn't know what they are begins to understand they
don't fit the system. *Book 2:* They advance far enough to see the ceiling — and
what is on the other side of it. *Book 3:* The filter breaks. Glory becomes the entry
point. They must go past it to survive what the integration brings.

---

## The Coming Fight — Series-Level Threat

### Not a villain. Competition.
The threat is not a monster or a tyrant. It is the Glory Realm's standard, which
is orders of magnitude above anything the civilization has produced at its best
single-door Glory practitioners.

Other power-filled worlds have been at or above Glory-equivalent for centuries.
They have their own ladders. They have been looking at the crack in the filter
with curiosity and patience.

When the filter breaks, the civilization joins the Glory Realm as the newcomers —
the weakest participants in a competitive ecosystem they have no map for.

### The rift-spawn fighters — what they already know
The rifts are not random thinning events. They are places where the filter's
crack is most advanced. The things that come through rift openings are not random
either: they are small-scale previews of what the full integration will bring.
The rift-spawn fighters have been reading the previews for 600 years.

They know:
- The scale of what is coming
- That the civilization's current maximum (single-door Glory) is insufficient
- That the fever dream is not a warning. It is a map.

### What the disk's fever dream shows
Rook's last clear memory before the EMP. He was at the research station.
The fMRI showed the magic practitioner and the Ember practitioner activating
the same brain region at the same moment. And then the EMP hit, and in the moment
the filter cracked, Rook saw — briefly — what was on the other side.

Not a monster. A world. Full of light, full of practitioners at levels he had
no language for. And something there that looked back.

The disk has carried that image for 600 years. The protagonist's fever dream
is Rook trying to pass it forward.

---

## The Protagonist's Unique Position

### Why the combined-path configuration matters at a series level
When the filter breaks and Glory becomes the floor, every single-door practitioner
will spend years adapting to the multi-door integration that Glory requires.

The protagonist began with all three doors open simultaneously. They have a
Glory-native configuration at the Kindled entry point. They have been practicing
the thing the entire civilization will need to learn from scratch.

This does not make Book 1 easy. It makes Book 1 necessary. The protagonist doesn't
know what they're training for yet. The disk does. Rook's memory knows. The
Touren lineage's deepest curriculum knows.

The protagonist's anomaly isn't a flaw in the system. It's a 600-year preparation.

---

## What the Training Is Actually For

The Kindling rank ladder (Kindled → Ember → Flame → Fire → Torch → Blaze → Glory)
was built as competition sport. The classification system, the brackets, the seasonal
competitions — this is how the civilization kept its practitioners advancing without
explaining the real stakes.

The real stakes:
- Glory = minimum viable to participate in the Glory Realm
- Past Glory = `RESERVED` — the next ladder, the next language, the next ceiling
- The rift-spawn fighters are training for past-Glory. No classification system
  covers what they're building toward.

Rook knew. The Touren lineage knows. The rift-spawn fighters know.
The classification system doesn't tell students this. It just tells them to advance.

