    95	Karis came first, at the seventh bell, from the refectory, by the library's front door and the main stair. She had come that way every evening of the year with her supper still in her and her notebook under her arm. Brom came second. Brom never took the main stair if there was another one. He went in at the library's north end and up the iron stair through the stacks, three flights of open treads that rang under a heavy man, because he said the long way round was the only exercise the library offered. Lira came third, by the front, slowly. And Seln, Cael knew without being told, would come last, up the bindery stair from the yard behind, the way the wing's man always came when he carried returned volumes up to the readers' shelves.
    96	
    97	Three ways onto the library's third floor. Then one stair from there to the top, the tight one, where two people could not pass. Everybody on the hill used one of the three. Nobody had ever thought about which.
    98	
    99	Cael sat at his sill with his coat on and Hesk's clock in front of him and waited for the third quarter after the bell.
   100	
   101	The clock lost time, as it always had. In tournament weeks he had set it an hour fast so that it would come right by accident, and on finals morning he had put it back himself. Tonight he had set it by the lecture range's bell at supper, and it would be a minute wrong by the eighth bell, perhaps two. He watched the minute hand anyway, because it was something to watch.
   102	
   103	When it stood at the quarter he got up, and named the door, and went.
   104	
   105	The court was dark and very cold.
   106	
   107	The fountain went on in the middle of it, as it did all year, with a crust of ice round the rim of the basin and the water going up black and coming down white in the light from the residence windows. He went round it at a walk, past the dark bulk of the lecture range and the records hall with its one lamp burning in the side window, which was Bracken's lamp and burned there every night of the year whether Bracken was under it or not.
   108	
   109	At the top of the library, four floors up, the reading room's window was lit.
   110	
   111	It was lit the way it was lit every night of the year, a yellow square at the top of the dark building, with the lamp set close to the glass so that you could see it from the north wall and the gate lodge and, Seln had once said, from the ferry landing at the foot of the bluff. The whole valley knew that window. A coat at the foot of the road could have told you the hour by it: lit at dusk, out somewhere between the tenth bell and the eleventh, never once dark before the ninth.
   112	
   113	Tonight it was lit exactly the same.
   114	
   115	He went in at the library's front door. *Door*, he told it, as his hand went to the iron ring. *Heavy. It keeps the cold out.* The lower hall was warm and smelled of lamp oil and old paper and the stove at the far end. Under the green-shaded lamps at the long tables two readers sat with their heads down. One was a fourth-year from the Ash faculty with a code open and his chin on his fist, and the other a girl Cael did not know, asleep with her cheek on her own notes. The night porter sat in his box by the stair with a newspaper. He looked up, saw a student going up to the reading rooms, and looked down.
   116	
   117	Three people. Not one of them would remember it tomorrow. He had caught the counting from Seln somewhere in the year without meaning to, and now he could not stop.
   118	
   119	The main stair went up in long shallow flights. On the second landing the ribs said what they thought of it. He stopped, and stood with one hand on the rail, and breathed, and went on. On the third floor the stair ended, and the tight one began, the one Karis called the top stair and the porters called worse things. He took it a step at a time. At the turn he passed the place where Lira would have had to stop too. There was nothing there to show it. She had been slow, and nobody had helped her.
   120	
   121	The top landing had one lamp on a bracket, and the annex door stood shut across it. The reading room's door had Bracken's card in its brass frame at eye height. *Reading room. Assigned: K. Dellenmoor.*
   122	
   123	He knocked once, and Karis let him in.
   124	
   125	---
   126	
   127	The table was bare.
   128	
   129	He had seen it covered for a year, and turned face down for one evening, and he had never once seen it bare. Every stack and every box and every volume of the code had gone. The long table stood under its old green cloth with nothing on it at all, and the cloth was faded in squares and oblongs where a year of paper had lain on it and kept the light off.
   130	
   131	"The annex floor," said Karis, before he could ask. "All of it, in order, and I'll be a week putting it back."
   132	
   133	She had kept the lamp in the window where it always was, turned up as high as every night, so that anybody looking up from the valley saw what they always saw. They were not sitting by it; a second lamp stood at the far end of the table, away from the glass, with a shade of brown paper pinned round it so that its light went down onto the cloth and nowhere else. The window seat was empty, and nobody would sit there tonight. A person in the window seat was a shape against a lit window, and Karis had thought of that before any of them.
   134	
   135	Brom had the chair by the shelf, with the sling still across his chest for one more night, and his spirit kettle stood on the end of the shelf over its little blue flame, not boiling. He nodded to Cael. He did not say anything about the stair or the stacks or the ribs.
   136	
   137	Lira had the bench, at the end of it, with her right leg out straight in front of her and her boot off, and the foot resting on its heel on the boards, and she saw him look at it.
   138	
   139	"It's fine," she said. "It's not fine, but it's better than it was. I came up the top stair on my backside for the last six steps, if you want to know, and nobody saw, because I waited till nobody was on it."
   140	
   141	"I didn't help you."
   142	
   143	"No, you didn't." She almost smiled. "Sit down. You're making the room smaller."
   144	
   145	He sat on the bench beside her. Karis sat across the table from them with her hands in her lap and the cord of her key round her neck and the key itself inside her collar. Nobody had brought anything. That was the strangest thing of all, in that room. Not a notebook, not a pen, not a cup. Karis had sent the assistant home at the sixth bell, she said, with the copying for the morning and a message for her mother, and the girl had gone down the stair singing.
   146	
