   148	
   149	Nobody talked much. Brom said the iron stair had a new loose tread on the second flight, the ninth, and he would tell the porter tomorrow. Lira said she had passed the Ash fourth-year on the main stair and he had asked after her ankle, and she had told him it was a horse, and he had believed her. Karis said nothing, but sat with her hands folded and looked at the bare cloth, the way she looked at a page before she began to read it.
   150	
   151	The kettle did not boil.
   152	
   153	The lecture range's bell gave the eighth hour, far off across the court, muffled by the shutters. Before the last stroke had died there was a single knock at the door, not loud.
   154	
   155	Karis got up and turned the key.
   156	
   157	Seln stood on the landing with his coat buttoned to the throat and something square under it, and he did not come in. He stood in the doorway and looked at the room in the order Cael knew. The door, the lock, the card; the two windows, the lit one with its lamp, the narrow one at the end, dark, with the library's roof below it; the landing behind him, the annex door, the stair; the bare table and the shaded lamp; then the four of them, one at a time.
   158	
   159	It took him longer than it had on the night of the birthday. Nobody hurried him.
   160	
   161	Then he came in and Karis locked the door behind him and hung the key back inside her collar. Seln went to the middle of the table, on the far side, where he always sat. He did not sit. He unbuttoned his coat and took out the case and set it down on the bare cloth in the circle of the shaded lamp.
   162	
   163	It was smaller than Cael remembered from the sill. Dark leather, scuffed at the corners, a brass lock and nothing else. No mark on the outside. There was a stamp inside the lid from the man who made it, Seln had said once, that nobody had ever seen but him.
   164	
   165	He took the key from his waistcoat pocket, a small brass key on a loop of black tape. He put it in the lock and turned it, and the lock opened with a sound like a dry twig, and he lifted the lid.
   166	
   167	He did it in four movements, the way a man sets out his tools on a bench before a job.
   168	
