   170	
   171	The second was a bundle tied in the wing's pink tape, about two fingers thick. He set it down at the end of the fourth row and did not untie it.
   172	
   173	The third was printed. Cael saw that at once from the way the light lay on it, flat and even, the way it never lay on a page anybody had written by hand. Seln laid it in two stacks side by side beyond the bundle. The left stack was yellower than the right.
   174	
   175	The fourth was a single leaf.
   176	
   177	It was in a sleeve of folded paper, plain and heavy, made to fit it. Seln took it out of the lid's pocket last, with two fingers at its edges. He laid it at the very end of the table by itself, a hand's width from everything else, and took his fingers away.
   178	
   179	Then he stepped back from the table and put both hands flat on the wood in front of him, and said nothing.
   180	
   181	Nobody said anything.
   182	
   183	Cael had no clock, because he had left Hesk's on the sill, and he did not count. He found afterward that he could not have told anybody how long it was, which had not happened to him since he was small. He knew only what he saw while it went on. The shaded lamp's flame stood up very straight and still in its glass. On the cloth the forty rows lay in its light, and the yellow stack, and the leaf in its sleeve at the end. Lira's hands were in her lap, one inside the other, and Brom did not reach for the kettle. Karis sat with her eyes going along the first row and back, along and back, the way they went along a shelf, and she did not reach for anything either.
   184	
   185	Seln's hands lay flat on the wood, and Cael watched them, and they did not move at all.
   186	
   187	At last Karis looked up.
   188	
   189	"May I?"
   190	
   191	"That is why they are on the table," said Seln.
   192	
   193	---
   194	
   195	She took the first one from the top of the first row. She drew the shaded lamp an inch nearer and held the two sheets under it, and Cael saw that they were both in the same hand, small and round and perfectly level, the copying hand of the wing that had filled the floor schedule on the refectory door every week for two years.
   196	
   197	"The first sheet is a copy of a station's Kindling record," said Karis. She read it in her plain voice, the one she used for a citation, giving each word its weight and no more. "*River station, the fourth district. Entry of the twelfth. A girl of the town. Reading at the frame: Current, second rank. Keeper's note at the margin: reads into Mire. Strong.*" She laid it face up in front of her. "That's the keeper's hand copied. You can see where he pressed."
   198	
   199	"The second?" said Brom.
   200	
   201	She held the clipped sheet out to him across the table. He took it in his good right hand and held it to the lamp, and read it out in his slow plain way, as if it were a bill of lading.
   202	
   203	"*Order of reclassification at evaluation. River station, the fourth district. Entry of the twelfth.*" He stopped, and found his place. "*Per corrective classification at Kindling evaluation: enter Current, Rank One. Margin reading not to be entered. Observe nine weeks following entry.*"
   204	
   205	He turned it over. There was a slip pinned to the back, a narrow strip in the same round hand, and he read that too.
   206	
   207	"*Observed nine weeks. Nothing to report.*"
   208	
   209	Nobody said anything.
   210	
   211	Karis took the second pair, and read the record, and Brom read the order. A station in the hill country, a boy. *Ash, first rank. Keeper's note: something of Stone in the frame; second family?* And the order: *Enter: Ash, Rank One. Margin reading not to be entered. Observe nine weeks.* And on the back, *Observed nine weeks. Nothing to report.*
   212	
