editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-cold-prompt.md:267:"*Order of reclassification at evaluation. River station, the fourth district. Entry of the twelfth.*" He stopped, and found his place. "*Per corrective classification at Kindling evaluation: enter Current, Rank One. Margin reading not to be entered. Observe nine weeks following entry.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-cold-prompt.md:405:"*Memorandum to keepers of stations and to clerks of entry. Corrective classification at Kindling evaluation.*" She put her finger under the line on the newest leaf. "And the body. It's three sentences, and it's been three sentences since this one." She touched the yellowest. "A word changes here and there, and *shall* became *is to*. A clause about the post came in when the post did. Otherwise you could lay the oldest on top of the newest and read straight through them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-cold-prompt.md:459:"*Of the advisory corrections function. The function is appended to the reading and is not the reading. It does not alter what the reading shows. It advises what is to be entered, and the entry is made as advised. It is to be maintained, and its maintenance is the office's charge.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-cold-prompt.md:481:He thought of Denvash, which he did not often let himself do. A district office on a cold morning, a chalk circle on boards worn into two shallow places where a generation of feet had stood, and a warden-adjunct with tired grey eyes three feet away at a desk with a brass plate let into it. Something had woken behind his breastbone, small, a point of light at the edge of his sight that he could feel and never point to. He had counted eleven seconds while the plate did not light, and then the word had come, very far away and very close, and the plate lighting late with the same word in it, and the light at the edge of his sight going out like a pinched wick.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-cold-prompt.md:483:All his life since, he had said that his Arbiter had spoken once and gone dark. He had said it to panels and wardens and Magisters, and written it in two books. It had been the one fixed fact at the bottom of every argument he had ever won. It had read him once, and said one word, and the registry had written down that word because it had nothing else to write, and he lived in the gap the word left.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-cold-prompt.md:485:The leaf said there were two things in that office, not one. There was the reading, and there was something appended to it. And there had been eleven seconds.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-cold-prompt.md:621:He told the circle chalked on the worn boards of a district office, and the warden-adjunct three feet away watching the brass plate let into her desk, and the thing that woke behind his breastbone and came up to the edge of his sight. He told the eleven seconds he had counted while the plate did not light, and the one word that came at the end of them, very far away and very close, and the light at the edge of his sight going out like a pinched wick and never coming back when he looked. He told the letter that had made Denvash impossible for Hesk's sake, and the road east. He told Lira next, because Lira had come next: six weeks of bouts in a yard at Ardenmere, with her knocking him down on the boards between them more times than either of them had counted. Then one cold night on a cot under a window, with the Log on his knee, something had been written in the dark place behind his breastbone where the light had gone out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-cold-prompt.md:687:"No frame has ever read anything off me but what I put in front of it. The plates in the wing read what I give them, and I give them the band. The read at Brom's grade is a surface, and I can hold a surface. And my own Arbiter went out at Denvash after one word. It's never lit since. I've not stood in a station since."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-cold-prompt.md:1326:"The leaf says the function is appended to the reading and advises what's entered. It doesn't say what happens when the reading gives it nothing to advise, and nothing else in the case says it in words." She looked at Cael. "But your file says it. The petition put it in schedule two by its full title, on the first night of the year, and you read it. *Suppression-Advisory Watch, Priority Level 4.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-cold-prompt.md:1330:"Advisory, the same word, on a designation nobody at any height has ever been able to trace to an office." Karis drew a breath. "Cael told me on the third night, when he brought the bread. I asked him about Denvash, and he told me: eleven seconds. The plate lit eleven seconds late, and the word came at the end of them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-cold-prompt.md:1334:"Eleven seconds is a long time for a reading," said Karis. "Every other reading on the continent is done before you've finished a breath. I think the function had his reading in front of it for eleven seconds and couldn't find a column to put it in, any column, and couldn't take the margin off because it was all margin. So it did what a good form does when it can't be filled in: it failed safe, and wrote the one word it had for a thing that couldn't be written. Then it marked the file, so that whoever came after would know there was something there it hadn't been able to correct." She laid the slip down. "The mark has no office behind it because no office made it. It made itself, the way a stamp comes down when a lever's pulled. And Ilsev sent it up, at the hearing, twice, to every height she could reach, and asked who'd authorized it. Something answered her each time, something that wasn't anybody."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-cold-prompt.md:1480:"I've thought about it every night this week, who you'd file it with. The seat runs on it, and so does the registry. Every evaluation office in the Compact sends its readings through it. You'd be filing a complaint about the corrections function with an office whose whole business is entering what the corrections function advises. And they'd read it, and they'd find nothing irregular in it, because there isn't anything irregular in it. Every form's been filled in properly for two hundred years." She took her hand off the notebook. "There isn't a seat outside it. There's nobody to send it to who isn't standing on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-cold-prompt.md:1620:*Four streets back from the seat, on the wharf side. The tall house with the brass plate. The third floor. The second bell after noon. Neither office's. Nothing written.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-cold-prompt.md:1640:The lane was narrow and steep, with a gutter down the middle and the backs of the warehouses on one side and a row of tall old houses on the other, let out floor by floor to clerks and factors and men who did a trade in paper. The building was the fourth along, with a door with a brass plate beside it listing its tenants, and a keeper's window, and a stair. Seln was at the keeper's window when Cael came in, writing his reason in the book, and did not look round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-cold-prompt.md:1662:"He came in by the front, not the yard. He walked up the lane from the wharf end, on the gutter side, in grey, with nothing in his hands, and he stopped and read the brass plate by the door, and then he went in past the keeper's window, where the keeper could see him." Seln looked at the door. "By the clock on the landing, eleven minutes early."
