editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-008/pre-repair/chapter-49.md:101:"Section one also says what a primary classification is. *The classification returned by the practitioner's Arbiter at Kindling and entered in the registry, including any entry made in the Supplementary Register.*" He did not hurry. "Mine was returned at Kindling. It took eleven seconds. It's entered in the Supplementary Register, statute nineteen, with an incidence count. I have a primary classification. It isn't one the standard framework can use, which is why section four's first half fits me. But section four lets you enter a practitioner into the category, and section one says who the category is for. It's for practitioners without one. Not being assessable by ordinary means isn't the same as not being classified."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-009/review-cold.md:13:Things I could not place and had to carry on trust: "the word on a brass plate in Denvash" (58, 59), "the five things kept from Hesk", Corvane (who taught him to draw a man), Ilsev, Amrit's, "the old burn" on the forearm, the earlier trough scenes, and the exact meaning of "the step". None of these stopped me; all of them were used in ways that told me their shape. The `FRAGMENT NOTICE` block in 58 was the one moment I felt the floor of an earlier book under my feet: I understood it as a system that watches him, and that this is a genre convention established long before, but a reader arriving here gets it as a cold object. That is acceptable for a last movement.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-009/review-cold.md:93:- *"What are you?" / "I don't fully know yet."* The movement withholds correctly. The word on the brass plate is never given. Vell deliberately does not write *Atypical*. Nothing here reads as a leak.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-008/review-cold.md:11:**Orientation, arriving at chapter 48 mid-book.** I was oriented within two pages. The chalked post with its three columns told me the mechanics at once: a boy (Cael, fourteen, though the number does not appear until ch51) has a "step" he can ask for, it costs him, and Lira makes him account for the cost. I picked up the rest by use rather than explanation: the Cinder House and Vell's book, Torvin's lodging house, Hesk in Denvash, the Compact's post at the top of the fish steps, Coss, an objection filed in the autumn, a "second notice" that arrived inside him after the Feryn loss. Terms I never fully understood but never needed to: Kindling, Arbiter, instance, statute nineteen, Fourthweek, "the plate lit eleven seconds late." The one word that cost me real friction is "the post," which means Lira's chalked post in the yard and also the Compact building at the head of the fish steps; the sentence "Not the post, either; it's Vell's" (113) and then "at the post, Ardenmere" (193) made me stop and re-sort. By ch49 it was clear. The opening sentence, "Properly, it turned out, meant a column," is an in-joke on a prior line I have not read; it reads as a wink I was not party to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-008/pre-repair/chapter-48.md:71:He had copied the three lines letter by letter from the back of the Log, and checked them twice against the page, and then once more against the place in the dark behind his breastbone where they had been written first.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-008/pre-repair/chapter-48.md:285:The category was not for anybody whose classification could not be read. It was for people who did not have one. Section four gave an assessor the power to put a practitioner into the category, and section one, three pages earlier, said who could be in it. A boy whose Arbiter had returned nothing would be in it. A boy whose return had been lost, or never recorded, would be in it. And a boy whose Arbiter had taken eleven seconds and then returned a word, a real word, a word with four instances and a statute of its own in the Supplementary Register, and a brass plate that had lit eleven seconds late, had a primary classification. He had more of one than anybody in the district. It simply was not one the standard framework could use.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-008/pre-repair/chapter-48.md:295:*Three. Section one also says what a primary classification is: what the Arbiter returned, entered in the registry, including the Supplementary Register. Mine was entered there. Statute nineteen. Four instances. It resolved; the plate lit.*
