editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-001/pre-repair-r2/chapter-03.md:1:# Chapter 3 — Eleven Seconds
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-001/pre-repair-r2/chapter-03.md:67:Pellin found the file, checked a line with her pen, and made a small mark. "Your Kindling assessment. Standard process: your Arbiter will resolve here, in front of the registry ledger." She tapped a flat brass plate set into the desktop at her elbow, dull and unremarkable, about the size of a book. "When your result reaches the Registry, this lights, and I record what it shows. The resolution itself is usually done before you've finished a breath; with the paperwork, the whole appointment takes between three and fifteen minutes. Any questions before we begin?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-001/pre-repair-r2/chapter-03.md:87:It did not come from outside him. That was the first thing, and none of the eleven descriptions on page seven had told him so. He had imagined something arriving, the way light arrives through a window; instead it gathered from inside him, from somewhere behind his breastbone, as though it had always been there and had only now decided to be noticed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-001/pre-repair-r2/chapter-03.md:91:He glanced at Pellin. She was three feet away, watching the brass plate, and her face showed nothing, because she could not see it. Nobody could. It was his, and it had always been his. It was his Arbiter.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-001/pre-repair-r2/chapter-03.md:101:By now, he understood, the plate should have lit; Pellin had said it was usually done before you finished a breath, and he had finished one. He watched her face from the corner of his eye, because her face was information. She was not looking at him. She was looking at the brass plate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-001/pre-repair-r2/chapter-03.md:127:He had been counting for eleven seconds. He was quite certain. He had counted every one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-001/pre-repair-r2/chapter-03.md:135:The brass plate at Pellin's elbow lit, eleven seconds late. Text rose up in it, pale and even, and settled into the same word he had just heard.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-001/pre-repair-r2/chapter-03.md:147:She did not say the word. Cael understood why, even then, standing in the circle with eleven seconds of silence still sitting in his chest. It was not unkindness. The word had already arrived and they both knew it, and saying it aloud would only have been for form, and she was not a person who did things for form.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-001/pre-repair-r2/chapter-03.md:171:He opened his notebook to the page with the date and the time, and under them he wrote *Eleven seconds*, and closed it again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-006/review-cold-prompt.md:1823:Brom walked out across the grey stone to the table. His forearms were still wound in linen from wrist to elbow, and he carried them a little out from his sides. The clerk lifted the plaque off the grey cloth and gave it to him. It was a plain square of dark wood with a brass plate let into it, the tournament's bar-and-ring cut at the top and a line of small type underneath.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-001/pre-repair-r2/chapter-04.md:37:The seal was dark wax pressed with the Compact's mark, a hand holding a balance, so cleanly that a machine must have done it. Cael thought, for no good reason, of the brass plate on Pellin's desk, and all that care poured into the outside of things. He broke the seal and unfolded the paper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-008/recheck-r1.md:105:- *Her knowledge:* she knows her own assessed ceiling and the facilities office's margin from the brass plate on every sill ("the rating was always her own assessed ceiling and a margin", ch49), she has read the protocol ("You read everything", the head; her finger "ahead of his voice" down Umber's list, ch50), and the risk officer's terms are hers to repeat ("Masts at your ceiling. The barrier up. The rows back. A floor rated for you, on a frame", ch49). Nothing she says exceeds that.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/review-cold-prompt.md:1608:Eleven seconds, all told. The hood received it. Jent did not stir. The counsel did not stir. There was nothing anybody on that platform could say about it, because there was nowhere in the schedule to say it. The clerk set the sheet on the pile of things received and picked up his next.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-001/review-cold.md:39:| Interest / freshness | 8 | Joren's heel (p.440–470); the eleven seconds (p.1148–1194) | Specific, concrete, and not like other books this reader has read: a superpower defeated by reading the fine print; a magic ceremony experienced as counting. Both are memorable at the level of "tell a friend about it." | High |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-001/review-cold.md:57:| Clarity / flow | 7 | Kindling mechanism (p.99 vs p.1122); Alis "classification as arriving in her chest before her ears" (p.1188, unsupported by p.305); Pellin's "three and fifteen minutes" (p.1104) vs a resolution that is "late" at eleven seconds (p.1168–1194) | Prose is clear. The rule questions land at the moments of highest pressure, which is exactly where a genre reader will stop and frown: in the circle, and at the notice. Each is a one-sentence fix. | Medium |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-001/review-cold.md:60:| Action / suspense | 7 | Reel bout as craft lesson (p.828–902); eleven seconds (p.1148–1194); cart (p.2360–2392) | Quantity is modest; quality is high. The reel bout is a real contest with legible adaptation ("A trick works once. After that, it's a habit"), and the eleven seconds is suspense by metronome. The cart is the only hero-action and is correctly withheld from explanation ("He was sure of that. He was nearly sure"). The owner's developed action should stay as written. | Medium-high |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-001/review-cold.md:85:- Pellin: "The process takes between three and fifteen minutes" (p.1104); but the plate "had not lit when it should have" by second seven (p.1168) and the resolution is "eleven seconds late" (p.1194). The normal resolution time is never stated; the reader infers it is near-instant, which makes Pellin's minutes refer to the whole appointment. One clarifying clause would close this. Confidence medium.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-001/review-cold.md:132:- **Observed issue:** (a) ch1 implies the Path wakes independently of the appointment; ch3 shows the circle as the trigger. (b) the norm for resolution time is never stated, so "eleven seconds late" has nothing on the page to be late against. (c) Alis never said that on the page. (d) the manuscript never says why Cael cannot live in an Unranked quarter of Denvash; the reason exists in pieces.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-001/review-cold.md:135:- **Strength to preserve:** the counting in the circle (p.1148–1184) and Pellin's hand resting on the drawer handle without opening it (p.1176). The eleven seconds works; it only needs a baseline so the reader knows what eleven is being measured against.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-009/review-editorial-prompt.md:633:He had not meant to. It was not in the plan; nothing borrowed was in the plan, and he and Lira had settled that on the first morning of the straw post without either of them having to say it. But it had been sitting in him since Feryn's wall like a question at the end of a page. There were two of them now, side by side in the dark behind his breastbone, the leaning one and the heavy one. He had only ever picked up one at a time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-009/review-editorial-prompt.md:1151:He had not asked for it. There was no time to ask for anything, and he had not asked for it since the trough. It came up out of the dark behind his breastbone as it had come in the sleet, already done before he knew it was happening, and the yard was a hand's width narrower than it had been. His weight was not where his heel had been. It was round the edge of the blow, on Darrow's left, at an angle no step of his own could have got to from where he stood. The straight went through the space where his chest had been, close enough that he felt it go by along his shirt.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-009/review-editorial-prompt.md:1213:He would go back over it a great many times, on the cot, and in the grey half, and on the wall by the pump, and it never once came out with a choice in it. His weight was going round on the Wind, and it went further than the Wind went. And in the dark behind his breastbone, beside the leaning thing, the heavy one was there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-009/review-editorial-prompt.md:1341:Cael felt the quiet all round him, and three hundred people inside it waiting to carry something home. He thought of the Log in his coat on the bench, open at last night's page. He thought of the line under *Evidence* with nothing after it but *Ruling*. He had filled that line in this afternoon, for the crossing. There was another line, under all of it, that had never had anything written beside it at all, since a word on a brass plate in Denvash, and there was nothing beside it now. Something in him had done a thing he could not describe. He had watched it happen from the inside, like a tenant watching the house he rents get up and walk.
