editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-001/review-editorial.md:67:3. **"behind his breastbone, behind his sternum"** (Ch3, the waking) — the same bone named twice as if they were two places. A narrator will read it as a stammer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-001/review-editorial.md:69:4. **The Arbiter dims but does not go out.** Ch3: "At the edge of his sight, the sigil dimmed. It did not go out. It settled, the way a cat settles into a chair it means to keep, and became something he could stop looking for." BOOK_MAP §1 (binding ending state) says "The Arbiter has been dark since the word," and §2.10 stages M3's courtyard attempt as "dark, absence not silence." A sigil that has settled into a chair it means to keep is presence, not absence. This is plan adherence rather than a verified canon error (the Bible says only that Arbiters "remain accessible"), but the plan line is marked binding and M3 depends on it. The fix is one sentence: let it go dark, or let it go where he cannot find it. The author report's own ledger line ("after the word it dimmed and settled at the edge of his sight") shows the author knows this is the state they wrote.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-001/review-editorial.md:79:8. **Kindling: spontaneous or at the appointment?** Ch1: "Your Path woke whether you wanted it or not. The only thing anyone actually scheduled was the certification appointment." Ch3: the Arbiter wakes when he steps into the circle, and the procedural summary says "Kindling is the moment it wakes." The Bible says Kindling is involuntary at fourteen and happens "regardless of whether the person is ready," which the prose honors in spirit (no way to skip it). The two statements can be reconciled (it wakes in the year you turn fourteen; the office is where it is done) but Ch1's phrasing suggests it could happen on its own in the street. A three-word clarification in Ch1 would settle it. Plan/clarity, not canon.
