editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-001/pre-repair/chapter-03.md:1:# Chapter 3 — Eleven Seconds
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-001/pre-repair/chapter-03.md:103:"Your Kindling assessment," she said. "Standard process. Your Arbiter will resolve here, in front of the registry ledger." She tapped a flat brass plate set into the desktop at her elbow. It was about the size of a book, dull, unremarkable. "When your result reaches the Registry, this lights. I record what it shows. The process takes between three and fifteen minutes. Any questions before we begin?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-001/pre-repair/chapter-03.md:131:That was the first thing. None of the eleven descriptions on page seven had told him that. He had imagined something arriving, the way light arrives through a window. Instead, it gathered from inside him, from somewhere behind his breastbone, behind his sternum, as though it had always been there and had only now decided to be noticed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-001/pre-repair/chapter-03.md:137:He glanced at Pellin. She was three feet away, watching the brass plate. Her face showed nothing. She could not see it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-001/pre-repair/chapter-03.md:153:He watched Pellin's face from the corner of his eye. Her face was information. She was not looking at him. She was looking at the brass plate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-001/pre-repair/chapter-03.md:183:He had been counting for eleven seconds. He was quite certain. He had counted every one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-001/pre-repair/chapter-03.md:191:The brass plate at Pellin's elbow lit.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-001/pre-repair/chapter-03.md:193:It was eleven seconds late. Text rose up in it, pale and even, and settled into the same word he had just heard.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-001/pre-repair/chapter-03.md:205:She did not say the word. Cael understood why, even then, standing in the circle with eleven seconds of silence still sitting in his chest. It was not unkindness. The word had already arrived. They both knew it. Saying it aloud would only have been for form, and she was not a person who did things for form.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-001/pre-repair/chapter-03.md:245:*Eleven seconds.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:43:"*Memorandum to keepers of stations and to clerks of entry. Corrective classification at Kindling evaluation.*" She put her finger under the line on the newest leaf. "And the body. It's three sentences, and it's been three sentences since this one." She touched the yellowest. "A word changes here and there, and *shall* became *is to*. A clause about the post came in when the post did. Otherwise you could lay the oldest on top of the newest and read straight through them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:97:"*Of the advisory corrections function. The function is appended to the reading and is not the reading. It does not alter what the reading shows. It advises what is to be entered, and the entry is made as advised. It is to be maintained, and its maintenance is the office's charge.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:119:He thought of Denvash, which he did not often let himself do. A district office on a cold morning, a chalk circle on boards worn into two shallow places where a generation of feet had stood, and a warden-adjunct with tired grey eyes three feet away at a desk with a brass plate let into it. Something had woken behind his breastbone, small, a point of light at the edge of his sight that he could feel and never point to. He had counted eleven seconds while the plate did not light, and then the word had come, very far away and very close, and the plate lighting late with the same word in it, and the light at the edge of his sight going out like a pinched wick.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:121:All his life since, he had said that his Arbiter had spoken once and gone dark. He had said it to panels and wardens and Magisters, and written it in two books. It had been the one fixed fact at the bottom of every argument he had ever won. It had read him once, and said one word, and the registry had written down that word because it had nothing else to write, and he lived in the gap the word left.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:123:The leaf said there were two things in that office, not one. There was the reading, and there was something appended to it. And there had been eleven seconds.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-009/review-cold-prompt.md:921:Cael wrote that down first, standing in the doorway of a chandler's that had not yet opened, on the fifth of the new month, with a fine rain deciding whether to begin. Two storeys of brown stone. A doorstep hollowed like a bowl by a hundred years of grain buyers coming in to argue. Beside the door, a brass plate about the size of a book, which said what the building was in letters too small to read from the far side of the street. A rope-maker's on one side of it, with coils of new rope hung in the window like sleeping snakes, and on the other a shuttered house with a cat on its sill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-008/review-editorial-prompt.md:575:It never did, anywhere. Every room she had slept in since she was sixteen had a single pane of thick glass set in lead, with a small brass plate on the sill to say what it was rated for, and the rating was always her own assessed ceiling and a margin. The program's facilities office ordered them. She had stopped trying latches years ago.
