editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:119:Cael counted it. He could not help counting it. From the moment the pen touched the first column to the moment it lifted from the second, eleven seconds.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:121:He filed them where the other eleven lived. A circle of pale wood in an office at Denvash, on the morning of his Kindling, and a brass plate at Pellin's elbow that had stayed dark for eleven seconds before it lit, while a fourteen-year-old boy stood in the circle and did not breathe. He had thought then that eleven seconds was the longest any office would ever take to decide what he was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-003/pre-repair/chapter-20.md:1:# Chapter 20 — Eleven Seconds
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-003/pre-repair/chapter-20.md:97:Eleven seconds. Cael had counted them in his own breath without meaning to, from the table's first word to its last, the walks back to chalk left out. Three exchanges. Four bursts. And his own hip, forty feet up and three years away from where it had first learned this, ached with each of them in turn, as if it had been asked to pay too. It knew the whole bill: toll at the front, jolt at the back, and the hot line from crest to inner thigh that would be written out on her by suppertime and still legible at breakfast. She had known that price at breakfast. She had read Nyle's page over his shoulder and decided, between two mouthfuls of bread, what the bout was worth to her, and it had been eleven seconds, and she had spent precisely that and kept the rest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-003/pre-repair/chapter-20.md:105:It was not the hush of people who have been impressed. Impressed was loud, a roar and a stamping. Cael had heard this other quiet twice before, both times in the Ironyard, both times on nights when something happened on the boards that the crowd had no place in its head to put. Every person in that yard had been handed the sheet that morning and had believed it, as you believe a printed timetable. They had trusted that ninety-one names had been put in their right order by people who knew. And now, in eleven seconds, in front of all of them, the sheet had been caught out about one name, beyond any argument, and every one of them knew it at once, and not one of them could have said what it was they knew.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-003/pre-repair/chapter-20.md:113:Down below the long board, where the results clerk was already reaching up with his chalk, Fiske had not moved. She had not moved for the whole eleven seconds, and she did not clap now. She stood with her hands at the small of her back and looked across the floor at Lira, who was standing alone in the middle of it with one palm pressed flat to her own thigh and her face showing nothing at all. And Fiske dipped her chin, once, a very small movement, made to nobody. It was the nod you give a guest you have been laying a place for, year after year, without ever knowing whose face would be in the doorway.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-002/EVENT-LIST.md:66:- Day 40: thirty-one on the steps counted from the coach; the side door's blacked hinges; the officer with the cold; the door log, eleven seconds, Pellin's plate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:91:*Four streets back from the seat, on the wharf side. The tall house with the brass plate. The third floor. The second bell after noon. Neither office's. Nothing written.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:111:The lane was narrow and steep, with a gutter down the middle and the backs of the warehouses on one side and a row of tall old houses on the other, let out floor by floor to clerks and factors and men who did a trade in paper. The building was the fourth along, with a door with a brass plate beside it listing its tenants, and a keeper's window, and a stair. Seln was at the keeper's window when Cael came in, writing his reason in the book, and did not look round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:133:"He came in by the front, not the yard. He walked up the lane from the wharf end, on the gutter side, in grey, with nothing in his hands, and he stopped and read the brass plate by the door, and then he went in past the keeper's window, where the keeper could see him." Seln looked at the door. "By the clock on the landing, eleven minutes early."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-003/pre-repair/chapter-21.md:187:*Wind. Copper.* On a door in the second quadrangle. Eleven seconds, eight hundred people, a Stone Path on his back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:123:The hood received it. The clerk laid the sheet on the pile of things received and reached for the next. Eleven seconds.
