editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-005/review-cold-prompt.md:663:"Through the door. With a key or something as good as one." She tapped the end of her drawn room. "The inner door has a counting lock. The Compact makes every chartered records office on the continent fit one. Each time the bolt goes across, it knocks a little brass plate inside the case, and the registrar reads the plate. Bracken reads his every Fifth-day, never oftener, because a registrar seen reading his lock plate on an odd day is a registrar who's telling the building he expects a thief. He read it yesterday anyway. Two marks. This morning, four." She held up four fingers, then folded two of them down, then the other two. "In and out. In and out. Two nights."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-009/review-cold.md:75:**Reserved disclosures — apparently intact.** "[SHATTERED]", "Suppression-Advisory Watch, Priority Level 4", "digestion", "the still place from session nine", "the debt it was sealed for", Seln's locked case and his unwritten closing word, Hesk's clock set back — all withheld in-world and all flagged as withheld. If any of these were meant to stay out of this book entirely, that is a plan question I cannot judge without the plan; as written, 60's room scene reads as the author's intended end-of-book reveal rather than a leak.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-007/pre-repair/chapter-41.md:17:He had been putting off this page for three days. Every night he had opened the back section and looked at the white paper under the last entry, and closed it again, because what he wanted to write was not a fact. Everything else in the back of the book was a fact. The cart was a fact; the drop was a fact; the notice, with its plain words in the dark behind his breastbone, was the plainest fact of all. What he wanted to write tonight was a guess, and he had told Lira once, on a step under a lantern, that guessing was how you wrote the wrong thing down and then trusted it because it was written.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-007/pre-repair/chapter-41.md:33:The wheezing man had begun his long whistle by the door. Yeni's lamp was down to a bead, and when he looked up she had turned on her side with her face to the wall, which meant either that she was asleep or that she had decided to let him think so. He put the Log back on the shelf beside Hesk's leather book and lay down on his right side, the side that did not catch, and lay awake for some time with the hip aching and the dark place behind his breastbone quiet and a little less dark than it had been.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-007/pre-repair/chapter-41.md:111:If it was anybody, and not only Lira, then it was everybody. Everybody he had ever watched hard enough. Renn's figure. Dessa's patience. The kiln boy's held breath, Sarel's closing, Talis planting where he could not see him go, Corbin reading feet at the mark. The woman with the shoulder doing her six positions in the old man's yard, every evening for three months, while he walked past. Any of them might have left something behind in him, quietly, the way the lock had left its shape in the key, and it might be sitting there now in the dark behind his breastbone with no notice on it yet, waiting for the right moment to come up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-007/pre-repair/chapter-41.md:177:He knew what he was looking for, this time, or he thought he did. He went to the dark place behind his breastbone, the place that was not quite as dark as it had been, and found the Wind there. He did not find a word or a picture, or anything he could have drawn; it was more a sort of leaning in the dark, a readiness, the shape the step had left when it went through him. It was the thing he reached for by the cot every night. He did not reach for it now. He went past it, carefully, as you go past a sleeping dog, and felt about in the dark beyond it for anything that was not Lira's.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-002/pre-repair/chapter-13.md:125:Cael counted it. He could not help counting it. From the moment the pen touched the first column to the moment it lifted from the second, eleven seconds.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-002/pre-repair/chapter-13.md:127:He filed them where the other eleven lived. A circle of pale wood in an office at Denvash, on the morning of his Kindling, and a brass plate at Pellin's elbow that had stayed dark for eleven seconds before it lit, while a fourteen-year-old boy stood in the circle and did not breathe. He had thought then that eleven seconds was the longest any office would ever take to decide what he was.
