editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-12.md:217:He thought about the word the registry had given him in a hall at fourteen, with the Arbiter gone dark over his head after eleven seconds. Somebody had done a sum about him too, in front of everybody, and written the answer down, and he had never once thought of answering it. He had only been getting on with things, a page at a time, in three columns, in the dark. Now, sitting beside her on the step, he wondered whether that was the same thing she meant, done more quietly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/compiled-prompt.md:456:Eleven seconds, all of it. While it went by, Cael's mind did a thing it often did in that room and went backward without asking him: to the side door an hour ago, where the young officer with the ruled board had lost his cold, and had not needed to ask the last name on his list because somebody had already copied it into the door log from the last sitting, *Caelen Hesk-ward*, so that all he had to write was the standing, *the enrollee of record, unclassified*, which took him nine seconds now that his hand knew the way. To the long floor, where Cael had crossed *nine*, *ten*, *eleven* and sat down with *seventeen* under his left foot for the third time, and known this time that he had walked to it on purpose. You could not choose the room. You could choose where you stood in it. And further back, to the ferry, where Karis had written one word on a slip and folded it twice and handed it to Brom.
