editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/state/movement-001/review-cold-prompt.md:1009:He took her hand and came up. He was beginning to feel it now, the thing the bursts cost that was not in the hip or the breath: a hollowness behind his breastbone, as though somebody had scooped out a cupful of whatever was in there and not put anything back. Seven bursts in less than an hour. He had never spent seven in an hour in his life.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/state/movement-001/review-cold-prompt.md:1051:"I can feel it." He could. The hollow behind his breastbone had widened, and it had a weight to it now, which made no sense, since it was an emptiness; it sat in his chest like a cold stone made of nothing. His hip throbbed with his heart. His breath kept wanting to stop on its own, halfway in, as though it had got the habit. "I said ten."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/state/movement-001/review-cold-prompt.md:1113:He woke before light with the hollow behind his breastbone grown into something he could almost hold, a cold weight of nothing that sat on his chest like a cat. His breath kept stopping by itself halfway in. His left hip had set in the night into a single long line of ache from the crest of the bone to the inside of the knee, so that he went down the stairs one at a time with his hand on the wall. The collarbone had a mark on it the shape of a staff end. The shoulder would not lift above the level of his ear.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/state/movement-001/review-cold-prompt.md:1121:He had not known, until that day, how much of him was the bursts. He had thought of them as a thing he did with his hip, as a man thinks of whistling as a thing he does with his mouth; but the hollow behind his breastbone did not feel like the hip, or the breath. It felt like the place he went to when the floor went short, the dark place behind the bone where the notices came, and it felt as though he had gone to it too often in too short a time and worn a hole in the floor of it. By the middle of the afternoon he could not have called the burst if his life had hung on it. He knew, without trying, that it was not there to call. It would come back. He was nearly sure it would come back. He lay on his bed and was nearly sure for several hours.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/state/movement-001/review-cold-prompt.md:1179:He found four other lines like it, once he started looking. A note about the hollow behind his breastbone sat at the bottom of a page about Ulric's heel. A line about the giving face of the Pressure, which he had used against a Copper formal in a hot week and which had taken the man's guard down like a door off its hinges, was jammed into the margin of a page about Dace's rings. One of Lira's sentences about the lock was in both books, in slightly different words, and he could not now remember which he had written first, or which was nearer to what she had actually said. That last one bothered him most. He had told himself, when he bought the slim book, that it would never hold a word it had not earned. And here was a sentence of Lira's living in two places in two shapes, so that if he ever needed to stand on it he would not know which of them would hold his weight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/state/movement-001/review-cold-prompt.md:1509:He knew it before he knew what it was. He stopped in the middle of a step, with his weight half moved, and his body was aware of it the way a body is aware of a change in the weather, before the head has noticed the clouds. It was not pain. It was not tiredness. It was the hush that came before a whole thought arrived, and he had felt it only three times in his life, and every time something had been written afterward in the dark place behind his breastbone.
