editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-004/review-editorial-prompt.md:3094:**Fragments & progression.** None. Arbiter not consulted (dark since the word; no attempt staged — the courtyard is M3's). Circuit: **unrated, 0–1** (loss to Renn, Cu3 Blade, fourth exchange, by the flat, called). A return bout with Renn is requested at a month, "inclined to give it". **Instance count:** none logged. The Renn third exchange is filed "whole and unexamined", and the cart sits beside it uncounted ("he did not let himself count them, because counting them would have been the first step toward a word"). M3's sparring plant can be "first instance" as planned.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-004/review-editorial-prompt.md:3175:- No fragments, no notices. The Arbiter is **dark**: absence, tested at length on the night of day 28.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-004/review-editorial-prompt.md:3353:At age 14, every person undergoes Kindling: the moment their latent Path potential activates. Kindling is involuntary — it happens regardless of whether the person is ready. An **Arbiter** appears at the moment of Kindling: a small glowing sigil that only the Kindling person can see and hear. The Arbiter evaluates the person's internal architecture and assigns their Path classification.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-004/review-editorial-prompt.md:3375:As a practitioner advances through ranks, new abilities manifest — presented by the Arbiter as visible ability declarations, experienced as text-like constructs in the practitioner's perception. These are called **Path declarations** and are specific to the practitioner's Path type.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-004/review-editorial-prompt.md:3483:- **The line is sharp, not a gradient:** a stride inside, the Arbiter is dark and no declaration renders; a stride outside, it is back — practitioners describe the return as a clock resuming (Lira's clock). Every Arbiter in a party fails identically, at the same stride, one way and back the same, regardless of Path or tier (B7 Ch10–11, Ch18, Ch21).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-003/AUTHOR-REPORT.md:80:- No fragments, no notices. The Arbiter is **dark**: absence, tested at length on the night of day 28.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-007/review-cold-prompt.md:1111:On the corner of the fish market there was a public house with two doors. The front one was propped open on a room full of wharfmen and smoke. The other, round the side, was shut, with a small brass plate screwed to it at the height of a man's eyes. Cael stopped and read the plate. It said that the back room was kept for ranked custom, and it gave the rank in a form of words that the registry had stopped using before Cael was born. Nobody had changed the plate. Nobody had needed to. Everybody who used that door knew what it meant.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/AUTHOR-REPORT.md:17:| 22 | Both Roads | 4,790 | 5 | Brom tips the chair: **"Prepare both roads." "Win the sittings. Keep a box."** — Lira counts three. The word *box* stays. Shirts, all the strapping, the plaque ("If I leave it, you'll go back for it"); hand to the LEFT pocket; **"It's the second box that's the problem."** — the sealed letter, twenty-three days; nobody asks. **Cael's first pole at full strength**, from the bottom: Pellin as a woman with a brass plate, eleven seconds, fourteen; forward through Vell, Greyvane, the grant and two renewals on paper, the inspection, Norhold's clause, Umber's *no number goes here*; "People change with the weather. Text doesn't." **"I'm not leaving the room while the text still works."** He pre-empts the objection; "always Karis who read it." **Karis names it an induction** (a run; "Every rule anybody ever broke looked exactly like this the evening before"; a tally, not a guarantee). **Lira: Fenmark's perfect form; "It's not ground. It's a floor."**; joists somebody laid; *Both roads* — it costs Cael nothing and nobody makes him pay. Seln's two looks. The slow spirit kettle; Karis: "Memory improves things"; **"It was the night we asked."** Seln lights them up the dairy step and crosses to the wing. Log. **SELN cutaway (~2,330), the dark wing**: no lamp, the fire-watch's square on the counter; his one correction examined (forty days as a narrow door); fifteen years, nineteen postings, sixty-three subjects, the followed few; the maintenance records copied twelve years ago on his fourth posting, understood in a green corridor between two appointments — *whose question it had been*; the boy inferred (the mill-town table; two slips burned on the coals; a season that never settled; Norhold spent on purpose); his own true, empty reports filed with the boy's receipts; the brass key held and returned; the dairy step and the lamp he held for each of them; ***The deciding has stopped being quiet.*** |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/AUTHOR-REPORT.md:34:- **The circle:** the road east as Lira's sum (the last Arbiter station four days past Ostrand loaded, three light; stamps stop there; two carters go past, the third turns at it and won't say why); the coastal default and *fled*; forty days after the fourth sitting's close; the five offices' inventories closed (Seln); Jent's sentence on the record; the fossil provision's two paragraphs (the second kept unmentioned); the escort order whole; a "facilities office" nobody can place; **Anchor Path** as a name and a use (rare; service; enforcement; holds people) — **inferred only: no range, rate or cost**; the three-way distinction (Ternhall's anchors / the Lattice Path / Anchor Path).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/AUTHOR-REPORT.md:144:- Pellin as a woman (ch22: "a woman with a brass plate"); the plate lit after eleven seconds (ch13).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/AUTHOR-REPORT.md:288:- **22:69** Denvash: brass plate → eleven seconds → stamped card → back door → three years to undo → **"Denvash," and he stopped; everyone knew the brass plate** (rest off-page).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/pre-repair-r3/chapter-26.md:103:They were from the dairy. He had written them on the way down in the coach that morning, out of habit, to have the argument by him in case anybody in that room should ever ask him for it: Denvash and the brass plate; Vell's ledger; the Greyvane hearing; the grant and two renewals on paper; the inspection; the clause at Norhold; Umber's *no number goes here*. His receipts. He had laid them out a week ago, in order, at full strength, and Karis had called them an induction and he had not had an answer for her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-47.md:121:Then the cost arrived, all at once and everywhere, in the shoulder first and then in the ribs on that side and then in a place behind his breastbone that he had not known could hurt.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-47.md:133:He did the third, and it worked again, a little less cleanly, the push going through him at a slightly wrong angle that he felt in the hip. After it he stood bent over with his hands on his knees, breathing, while the place behind his breastbone told him things.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-47.md:211:That evening, after Lira's hour, he did the redirect three more times against Brom's slow burst, in the lamplight, with the shoulder still remembering the first three. Then he sat on the bench with his eyes shut and the place behind his breastbone aching, and thought about the number on his hand, and saw that Brom was doing to the knock what Cael did to everybody else on a bench: changing one condition at a time to see where it broke. He had never once thought to do it to himself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-52.md:105:By Cael's private count the exchange had run four minutes and eleven seconds. He stayed sitting and watched Lira walk back to her chalk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-52.md:121:The fourth exchange lasted eleven seconds.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-52.md:145:The first belonged to Brom. The third exchange had held him on the iron for four minutes and eleven seconds, and the turn of the glass had not given that back; eleven seconds into the fourth, his turn came on the fifth count instead of the fourth, a hair late.
