editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-003/review-cold-prompt.md:1905:He thought about the circle in Pellin's office. The pale wood worn smooth in two places side by side. He thought about putting his feet where the wood was worn, and the thing that had gathered behind his breastbone, small, and risen, and come to rest at the edge of his sight, just off to one side. *Every practitioner's Arbiter is their own, dormant since birth.* The procedural summary had said that you reached it by attending to the place behind the breastbone. He had read that a dozen times at the long table under the window. He had never once had to do it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-003/review-cold-prompt.md:1907:He attended to the place behind his breastbone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-003/review-cold-prompt.md:1919:He opened his eyes and looked to the edge of his sight, left, where it had hung, steady as a nail, for eleven seconds. Then right. Then left again, quickly, the way you turn your head for a sound you are sure you heard.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-003/review-cold-prompt.md:1923:He tried again. And again. He counted. He had always counted, and he counted now, though he would have given a good deal not to. Four. Five. He held the reach longer each time, past the point where it felt reasonable, the way you hold your breath past comfort to find out how long you can, daring the dark behind his breastbone to prove him wrong. Six. Seven.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-003/review-cold-prompt.md:1927:And it was not silence. He knew that now, standing in the yard, as clearly as he had known it in the side room. Silence would have been something. Silence would have been a thing that was there and was choosing not to answer, the way Pellin's brass plate had sat dark for eleven seconds and then lit. He could have waited out a silence. He could have knocked louder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-003/review-cold-prompt.md:1945:Every one of them could reach into the dark behind their breastbone and find a light there, and the light would tell them what they were. Not all of it. Not the whole of a person. But the part that the city cared about. The part that decided which side of the bridge you slept on. Tier. Rank. Path. Whether you were getting better.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-003/review-cold-prompt.md:2041:"It came up the way everybody says. From behind here." He touched his breastbone. "It sat at the edge of my eye for eleven seconds. Then it said the word, and then it went out, like a lamp when you pinch the wick. And it hasn't come back." He made himself say the next part. "I didn't look for it again for four weeks. I was afraid to. Last night I went down into Torvin's yard at midnight and tried. Properly. I don't know how many times. I stopped counting."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-003/review-cold-prompt.md:2249:It was all still there. The absence was still there behind his breastbone, exactly where it had been at midnight. The three things were still in their corner, one of them counted. The review was still on a shelf in Denvash, propped against a clock. Somewhere a polite man with boots from somewhere else still knew there was a circuit kid at Torvin's.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-49.md:95:It never did, anywhere. Every room she had slept in since she was sixteen had a single pane of thick leaded glass, with a small brass plate on the sill to say what it was rated for, and the rating was always her own assessed ceiling and a margin. The program's facilities office ordered them. She had stopped trying latches years ago.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-07.md:141:He knew it before he knew what it was. He stopped in the middle of a step, with his weight half moved, and his body knew it before his head did. It was not pain. It was not tiredness. It was the hush that came before a whole thought arrived, and he had felt it only three times in his life, and every time something had been written afterward in the dark place behind his breastbone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-42.md:73:Lira saw it too, a step later than he did. She stopped. She stood in a narrowing strip of honest wood with Fiske's slick on both sides of her and across her front, and every inch of the strip had been drawn, in eleven seconds, by a woman who knew where Lira's feet liked to go and had simply removed all of those places before Lira got there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-004/review-cold-prompt.md:245:Since his Kindling he had not had one day without somebody writing him down. Assessors, a panel, an Arbiter with a classification, a warden with a challenge in his pocket, and a semester evaluation waiting at the end of this one; grey coats at every gate and ferry on two roads; and now a whole hill that had bought his transcript at six silver marks a copy and read it before he walked through the gate. He had grown so used to it that he only noticed it now in the way a man notices his own coat when somebody else asks him to take it off.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-004/review-cold-prompt.md:731:It was not a bout anybody would remember. The name four places above her belonged to a Stone fifth-year who had stood near the top of the Copper sheet for two seasons by being very hard to move. He was not hard to move for long. Lira took him in three exchanges and three bursts, each touch arriving from a side his weight had just left, and the yard, which a fortnight ago had gone silent at eleven seconds, clapped politely and went back to its bread and its arguments. That was a measurement too, and Cael wrote it down: a thing that had been impossible on the second session had become, by the tenth, merely expected.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-004/review-cold-prompt.md:1307:Each post was meant to be that something. Bolted to every plinth, in letters a quarter of an inch high, was a brass plate saying what happened if they ever failed to be.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-004/review-cold-prompt.md:1399:One second-year was off toward the warden's shed before the instructor had finished the word *warden*. The porter, sent to the wing for the duty clerk, went with his broom still on his shoulder. She moved everybody back out of the north bay with a single flat word, and nobody touched the post. One of the second-years, coming back, stood in front of it and read the brass plate aloud, every word, in a voice that was not steady, as though reading it might still help. Nobody had the heart to stop her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-004/review-cold-prompt.md:1821:A second declaration, three beats after the first, into the place the sequence went next. Lira broke off again, and the third went down behind her, into the stretch of floor the fold would have brought her through, and in eleven seconds there was nowhere on the boards that the sequence could reach. Lira stood in a narrow patch of honest wood with oil on three sides of it, and every edge of the patch had been drawn by Fiske, from the copy Lira herself had handed her a minute earlier.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-002/review-editorial-prompt.md:719:Cael thought about Pellin's office, the circle of pale wood, the brass plate that lit eleven seconds late. He thought about a form drawn from the back of a drawer, filled in with small quick strokes, and how nobody in that room had asked him anything, because there had not been anything to ask; the question had already been answered by someone who was not in the room.
