editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-002/pre-repair/chapter-09.md:105:Cael thought about Pellin's office, the circle of pale wood, the brass plate that lit eleven seconds late. He thought about a form drawn from the back of a drawer, filled in with small quick strokes, and how nobody in that room had asked him anything, because there had not been anything to ask; the question had already been answered by someone who was not in the room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-007/pre-repair/chapter-43.md:171:On the corner of the fish market there was a public house with two doors. The front one was propped open on a room full of wharfmen and smoke. The other, round the side, was shut, with a small brass plate screwed to it at the height of a man's eyes. Cael stopped and read the plate. It said that the back room was kept for ranked custom, and it gave the rank in a form of words that the registry had stopped using before Cael was born. Nobody had changed the plate. Nobody had needed to. Everybody who used that door knew what it meant.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:199:"If I gave you names I would be guessing, and I don't guess." He did not say it to be clever. He said it as Gault said a plate would not read past its last notch. "I can tell you what my year has looked like from my chair. A year ago I was asked what you are. This year I am asked what can be done about you. I answer every one of those letters by saying, again, what you are. Each answer goes up the stair. Each one is entered and filed. I am fairly sure none of them is read." He turned his left hand over, and back. "My office still works. It works very well. It has simply stopped mattering. When the questions stop being questions, an evaluator knows the decision has been taken in some room he was not shown."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/EVENT-LIST.md:35:2. Cael argues the first pole at full strength. Way in: Karis says "You're holding one back" before he's finished, and he starts again from the bottom: Denvash — a woman with a brass plate, eleven seconds, no text (Pellin as a woman per Book 1); then forward: Vell's ledger, the Greyvane hearing and the clause, the grant and two renewals on paper, an inspection that found nothing, a tournament their own clause put him in, an instrument whose keeper wrote that no number goes there. People change with weather; text doesn't; it's ground. [P] "I'm not leaving the room while the text still works." Lira: the seal said lawful and proceeding. He: lawful is forever; every corridor had a door nobody read. Brom: and if this code has none? Cael: then it's the first.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-003/pre-repair/chapter-20.md:59:"It came up the way everybody says. From behind here." He touched his breastbone. "It sat at the edge of my eye for eleven seconds. Then it said the word, and then it went out, like a lamp when you pinch the wick. And it hasn't come back." He made himself say the next part. "I didn't look for it again for four weeks. I was afraid to. Last night I went down into Torvin's yard at midnight and tried. Properly. I don't know how many times. I stopped counting."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-003/pre-repair/chapter-20.md:267:It was all still there. The absence was still there behind his breastbone, exactly where it had been at midnight. The three things were still in their corner, one of them counted. The review was still on a shelf in Denvash, propped against a clock. Somewhere a polite man with boots from somewhere else still knew there was a circuit kid at Torvin's.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/state/movement-001/pre-repair/chapter-06.md:9:He found four other lines like it, once he started looking. A note about the hollow behind his breastbone sat at the bottom of a page about Ulric's heel. A line about the giving face of the Pressure, which he had used against a Copper formal in a hot week and which had taken the man's guard down like a door off its hinges, was jammed into the margin of a page about Dace's rings. One of Lira's sentences about the lock was in both books, in slightly different words, and he could not now remember which he had written first, or which was nearer to what she had actually said. That last one bothered him most. He had told himself, when he bought the slim book, that it would never hold a word it had not earned. And here was a sentence of Lira's living in two places in two shapes, so that if he ever needed to stand on it he would not know which of them would hold his weight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/review-cold-prompt.md:387:"At Denvash a woman with a brass plate on her desk decided what I was in eleven seconds," he said. "There was nothing written down in that room. Nothing she had to answer to. The plate sat dark for eleven seconds and then lit, and she stamped a name on a card and sent me out of the back, and I was fourteen, and there was no paper anywhere that said she was wrong." He kept his hands still on the slate. "It took me three years to get one sentence written down that undid her. Everything good since has come the same way."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/review-cold-prompt.md:1369:They were from the dairy. He had written them on the way down in the coach that morning, out of habit, to have the argument by him in case anybody in that room should ever ask him for it: Denvash and the brass plate; Vell's ledger; the Greyvane hearing; the grant and two renewals on paper; the inspection; the clause at Norhold; Umber's *no number goes here*. His receipts. He had laid them out a week ago, in order, at full strength, and Karis had called them an induction and he had not had an answer for her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-20.md:1:# Chapter 20 — Eleven Seconds
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-20.md:103:Eleven seconds. Cael had counted them in his own breath without meaning to, from the table's first word to its last, the walks back to chalk left out. Three exchanges. Four bursts. And his own hip, forty feet up and three years away from where it had first learned this, ached with each of them in turn. It knew the whole bill: toll at the front, jolt at the back, and the hot line from crest to inner thigh that would be written out on her by suppertime and still legible at breakfast. She had known that price at breakfast. She had read Nyle's page over his shoulder and decided, between two mouthfuls of bread, what the bout was worth to her, and she had spent precisely that and kept the rest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-20.md:111:It was not the hush of people who have been impressed. Impressed was loud, a roar and a stamping. Cael had heard this other quiet twice before, both times in the Ironyard, both times on nights when something happened on the boards that the crowd had no place in its head to put. Every person in that yard had been handed the sheet that morning and had believed it, as you believe a printed timetable. They had trusted that ninety-one names had been put in their right order by people who knew. And now, in eleven seconds, in front of all of them, the sheet had been caught out about one name, beyond any argument, and every one of them knew it at once, and not one of them could have said what it was they knew.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/state/movement-003/review-editorial-prompt.md:1905:He thought about the circle in Pellin's office. The pale wood worn smooth in two places side by side. He thought about putting his feet where the wood was worn, and the thing that had gathered behind his breastbone, small, and risen, and come to rest at the edge of his sight, just off to one side. *Every practitioner's Arbiter is their own, dormant since birth.* The procedural summary had said that you reached it by attending to the place behind the breastbone. He had read that a dozen times at the long table under the window. He had never once had to do it.
