editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/state/movement-001/pre-repair/chapter-05.md:131:He took her hand and came up. He was beginning to feel it now, the thing the bursts cost that was not in the hip or the breath: a hollowness behind his breastbone, as though somebody had scooped out a cupful of whatever was in there and not put anything back. Seven bursts in less than an hour. He had never spent seven in an hour in his life.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/state/movement-001/pre-repair/chapter-05.md:173:"I can feel it." He could. The hollow behind his breastbone had widened, and it had a weight to it now, which made no sense, since it was an emptiness; it sat in his chest like a cold stone made of nothing. His hip throbbed with his heart. His breath kept wanting to stop on its own, halfway in, as though it had got the habit. "I said ten."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/state/movement-001/pre-repair/chapter-05.md:235:He woke before light with the hollow behind his breastbone grown into something he could almost hold, a cold weight of nothing that sat on his chest like a cat. His breath kept stopping by itself halfway in. His left hip had set in the night into a single long line of ache from the crest of the bone to the inside of the knee, so that he went down the stairs one at a time with his hand on the wall. The collarbone had a mark on it the shape of a staff end. The shoulder would not lift above the level of his ear.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/state/movement-001/pre-repair/chapter-05.md:243:He had not known, until that day, how much of him was the bursts. He had thought of them as a thing he did with his hip, as a man thinks of whistling as a thing he does with his mouth; but the hollow behind his breastbone did not feel like the hip, or the breath. It felt like the place he went to when the floor went short, the dark place behind the bone where the notices came, and it felt as though he had gone to it too often in too short a time and worn a hole in the floor of it. By the middle of the afternoon he could not have called the burst if his life had hung on it. He knew, without trying, that it was not there to call. It would come back. He was nearly sure it would come back. He lay on his bed and was nearly sure for several hours.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-43.md:149:On the corner of the fish market there was a public house with two doors. The front one was propped open on a room full of wharfmen and smoke. The other, round the side, was shut, with a small brass plate screwed to it at the height of a man's eyes. Cael stopped and read the plate. It said that the back room was kept for ranked custom, and it gave the rank in a form of words that the registry had stopped using before Cael was born. Nobody had changed the plate. Nobody had needed to. Everybody who used that door knew what it meant.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-60.md:5:Cael wrote that down first, standing in the doorway of a chandler's that had not yet opened, on the fifth of the new month, with a fine rain deciding whether to begin. Two storeys of brown stone. A doorstep hollowed like a bowl by a hundred years of grain buyers coming in to argue. Beside the door, a brass plate about the size of a book, which said what the building was in letters too small to read from the far side of the street. A rope-maker's on one side of it, with coils of new rope hung in the window like sleeping snakes, and on the other a shuttered house with a cat on its sill.
