editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-11.md:39:She did not use it; she did not have time. He was gone, a long clean step to her right, and her staff went through the air where he had been. But she had seen it, and she put it away somewhere behind her breastbone, carefully, for her own middle column. *He goes home first.* She would have that tomorrow, whatever happened tonight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-004/pre-repair/chapter-25.md:5:It was not a bout anybody would remember. The name four places above her belonged to a Stone fifth-year who had stood near the top of the Copper sheet for two seasons by being very hard to move. He was not hard to move for long. Lira took him in three exchanges and three bursts, each touch arriving from a side his weight had just left, and the yard, which a fortnight ago had gone silent at eleven seconds, clapped politely and went back to its bread and its arguments. That was a measurement too, and Cael wrote it down: a thing that had been impossible on the second session had become, by the tenth, merely expected.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/STATE_LEDGER.md:33:- Registry: [SHATTERED]; no tier. File carries Suppression-Advisory Watch, Priority Level 4 (Cael does not know this).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-35.md:101:It was very thin. Between the registry's stamp and the green slip on its first leaf, the classification block still carried the line with nothing in its column: *Suppression-Advisory Watch, Priority Level 4.* No officer had entered it, anywhere in the file or in the duty records of any floor. Nobody had dated it, and nobody had renewed it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-58.md:43:He had not asked for it. There was no time to ask for anything, and he had not asked for it since the trough. It came up out of the dark behind his breastbone as it had come in the sleet, already done before he knew it was happening, and the yard was a hand's width narrower than it had been. His weight was not where his heel had been. It was round the edge of the blow, on Darrow's left, at an angle no step of his own could have got to from where he stood. The straight went through the space where his chest had been, close enough that he felt it go by along his shirt.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-58.md:105:He would go back over it a great many times, on the cot, and in the grey half, and on the wall by the pump, and it never once came out with a choice in it. His weight was going round on the Wind, and it went further than the Wind went. And in the dark behind his breastbone, beside the leaning thing, the heavy one was there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-58.md:233:Cael felt the quiet all round him, and three hundred people inside it waiting to carry something home. He thought of the Log in his coat on the bench, open at last night's page. He thought of the line under *Evidence* with nothing after it but *Ruling*. He had filled that line in this afternoon, for the crossing. There was another line, under all of it, that had never had anything written beside it at all, since a word on a brass plate in Denvash, and there was nothing beside it now. Something in him had done a thing he could not describe. He had watched it happen from the inside, like a tenant watching the house he rents get up and walk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-58.md:321:It came as the others had come. There was the quiet first, the quiet before a thought arrives whole; then the turning over; then, in the dark place behind his breastbone where nothing had lit since the word, something written.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-09.md:107:Cael thought about Pellin's office, the circle of pale wood, the brass plate that lit eleven seconds late. He thought about a form drawn from the back of a drawer, filled in with small quick strokes, and how nobody in that room had asked him anything, because there had not been anything to ask; the question had already been answered by someone who was not in the room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-05.md:131:He took her hand and came up. He was beginning to feel it now, the thing the bursts cost that was not in the hip or the breath: a hollowness behind his breastbone, as though somebody had scooped out a cupful of whatever was in there and not put anything back. Seven bursts in less than an hour. He had never spent seven in an hour in his life.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-05.md:175:"I can feel it." He could. The hollow behind his breastbone had widened, and it had a weight to it now, which made no sense, since it was an emptiness; it sat in his chest like a cold stone made of nothing. His hip throbbed with his heart. His breath kept wanting to stop on its own, halfway in. It had got the habit. "I said ten."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-05.md:237:He woke before light with the hollow behind his breastbone grown into something he could almost hold, a cold weight of nothing that sat on his chest like a cat. His breath kept stopping by itself halfway in. His left hip had set in the night into a single long line of ache from the crest of the bone to the inside of the knee. He went down the stairs one at a time with his hand on the wall. The collarbone had a mark on it the shape of a staff end. The shoulder would not lift above the level of his ear.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-05.md:245:He had not known, until that day, how much of him was the bursts. He had thought of them as a thing he did with his hip, as a man thinks of whistling as a thing he does with his mouth. But the hollow behind his breastbone did not feel like the hip, or the breath. It felt like the place he went to when the floor went short, the dark place behind the bone where the notices came. He had gone to it too often in too short a time and worn a hole in the floor of it. By the middle of the afternoon he could not have called the burst if his life had hung on it. He knew, without trying, that it was not there to call. It would come back. He was nearly sure it would come back. He lay on his bed and was nearly sure for several hours.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-19.md:225:He called up the circle in Pellin's office, the pale wood worn smooth in two places side by side. He called up the feeling of putting his feet where the wood was worn, and the thing that had gathered behind his breastbone, small, and risen, and come to rest at the edge of his sight, just off to one side. *Every practitioner's Arbiter is their own, dormant since birth.* The procedural summary had said that you reached it by attending to the place behind the breastbone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-19.md:229:He attended to the place behind his breastbone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-19.md:245:He opened his eyes and looked to the edge of his sight, left, where it had hung, steady as a nail, for eleven seconds. Then right. Then left again, quickly, as you turn your head for a sound you are sure you heard.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-19.md:249:He tried again, and again, and he counted, because he had always counted, though he would have given a good deal not to. Four. Five. Each reach he held for longer than the one before, well beyond what was sensible, as a swimmer stays under after his chest has begun to ache, only to learn where the bottom of his breath is, and all the while the dark behind his breastbone lay there and let him. Six. Seven.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-19.md:253:And it was not silence. He knew that now, standing in the yard, as clearly as he had known it in the side room. Silence would have meant something there, choosing to keep quiet, as Pellin's brass plate had kept dark for eleven seconds before it lit. He could have waited out a silence. He could have knocked louder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-19.md:273:Every one of them could reach into the dark behind their breastbone and find a light there, and the light would tell them what they were. Not the whole of a person, but the part the city cared about, the part that decided which side of the bridge you slept on: tier, rank, Path, whether you were getting better.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/review-editorial-prompt.md:387:"At Denvash a woman with a brass plate on her desk decided what I was in eleven seconds," he said. "There was nothing written down in that room. Nothing she had to answer to. The plate sat dark for eleven seconds and then lit, and she stamped a name on a card and sent me out of the back, and I was fourteen, and there was no paper anywhere that said she was wrong." He kept his hands still on the slate. "It took me three years to get one sentence written down that undid her. Everything good since has come the same way."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/review-editorial-prompt.md:1369:They were from the dairy. He had written them on the way down in the coach that morning, out of habit, to have the argument by him in case anybody in that room should ever ask him for it: Denvash and the brass plate; Vell's ledger; the Greyvane hearing; the grant and two renewals on paper; the inspection; the clause at Norhold; Umber's *no number goes here*. His receipts. He had laid them out a week ago, in order, at full strength, and Karis had called them an induction and he had not had an answer for her.
