editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/state/movement-007/pre-repair/chapter-45.md:63:The rank was a measure of what an examiner could see in a hall on a given day. He knew exactly how much that was worth; he had been looked at by an Arbiter for eleven seconds once, and written down as a single word. Feryn and Sarel had come up on circuits and in yards, the hard way, with gifts and gaps both. He had beaten the ones he had beaten by finding the gap their gift had grown round. A man who had been in a paid yard at ten and an academy at eleven did not have gaps like that. Every gap he had ever had, somebody had been paid to find and close. What was left would be very small, and very well hidden, and the man would know where it was better than anyone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-21.md:37:He felt it want to, quite clearly, as though somebody had put a hand flat on his chest. A small cold current went up from his stomach through the middle of him and sat under his breastbone, and he knew the feeling, because he had last had it standing in a circle of pale wood on Weaver's Row while a brass plate failed to light.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-47.md:83:It came as the first one had, thirty-seven nights before, on this same cot. There was no sound and no light, and nowhere it could be said to come from. There was the quiet first, the hush in which a whole thought gets ready to arrive, and then a turning over, and then, in the dark place behind his breastbone where nothing had lit since the word, something written.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-47.md:169:He shut the book and set it on the shelf beside Hesk's leather one. He lay down very carefully on his back, which was the only way left that ought not to hurt, and it hurt anyway. He lay looking up at the beam with its old nail holes in the light of Yeni's lamp. Behind his breastbone, side by side in the dark, sat the two things, the one that leaned and the one that pressed, and he listened to the wheezing man begin to whistle.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-47.md:283:He thought about two lines of figures in the dark behind his breastbone where there had been one. There were two doors, perhaps, where he had thought there was only one, and he did not know where either of them went, or which of them he had come through, or whether there were others in the dark he had not found yet. He did not know what Kestrel had been, or what Feryn's hand had been, or whether Lira's three months and Feryn's one second were the same thing at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-41.md:17:He had been putting off this page for three days. Every night he had opened the back section and looked at the white paper under the last entry, and closed it again, because what he wanted to write was not a fact. Everything else in the back of the book was a fact. The cart was a fact; the drop was a fact; the notice, with its plain words in the dark behind his breastbone, was the plainest fact of all. What he wanted to write tonight was a guess, and he had told Lira once, on a step under a lantern, that guessing was how you wrote the wrong thing down and then trusted it because it was written.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-41.md:33:The wheezing man had begun his long whistle by the door. Yeni's lamp was down to a bead, and when he looked up she had turned on her side with her face to the wall, which meant either that she was asleep or that she had decided to let him think so. He put the Log back on the shelf beside Hesk's leather book and lay down on his right side, the side that did not catch, and lay awake with the hip aching and the dark place behind his breastbone quiet and a little less dark than it had been.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-41.md:99:If it was anybody, and not only Lira, then it was everybody he had ever watched hard enough. Renn's figure, Dessa's patience, the kiln boy's held breath, Sarel's closing, Talis planting where he could not see him go, Corbin reading feet at the mark: any of them. The woman with the shoulder, too, doing her six positions in the old man's yard every evening for three months while he walked past. Any of them might have left something behind in him, quietly, as the lock had left its shape in the key, and it might be sitting there now in the dark behind his breastbone with no notice on it yet, waiting.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-41.md:153:He went to the dark place behind his breastbone, which was not quite as dark as it had been, and found the Wind there. It was not a word or a picture, but a sort of leaning in the dark, the shape the step had left when it went through him. He did not reach for it. He went past it, carefully, as you go past a sleeping dog, and felt about in the dark beyond it for anything that was not Lira's. He put the whole morning in front of himself, the heels settling and the sack going up a half beat late and the tar on the big hands, and reached for whatever it had left.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/packets/MOVEMENT-008.md:35:  3. The watch reforms. **"Nothing was gained inside" is entered first.** "By count." Five go over stake thirty-four (Cael, then Lira, Karis, the young one, Vastin) and four stay on the shelf. They walk. The pinch. Eleven seconds. "You'll die in there." / "We've been in three times." The watch goes east one at a time and is not pursued.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-03.md:1:# Chapter 3 — Eleven Seconds
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-03.md:67:Pellin found the file, checked a line with her pen, and made a small mark. "Kindling assessment, the usual way. Your Arbiter wakes here, and the result goes straight to the registry ledger." She laid two fingers on a flat brass plate let into the desktop at her elbow, dull and unremarkable, about the size of a book. "When your result reaches the Registry, this lights, and I record what it shows. The resolution itself is usually done before you've finished a breath; with the paperwork, the whole appointment runs from three minutes to fifteen. Do you have questions first?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-03.md:87:It did not come from outside him; that was the first thing, and none of the eleven descriptions on page seven had told him so. He had imagined something arriving, as light arrives through a window; instead it gathered from inside him, from somewhere behind his breastbone, as though it had always been there and had only now decided to be noticed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-03.md:91:He glanced at Pellin, who was three feet away watching the brass plate, and her face showed nothing, because she could not see it and nobody could; it was his, and it had always been his, and nobody else would ever see it. It was his Arbiter.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-03.md:101:By now, he understood, the plate should have lit; Pellin had said it was usually done before you finished a breath, and he had finished one. He kept her face at the edge of his eye; her face was information. She was not looking at him but at the brass plate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-03.md:127:Eleven seconds. He was sure of the number. He had counted every one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-03.md:135:The brass plate at Pellin's elbow lit, eleven seconds late. Text rose up in it, pale and even, and settled into the same word he had just heard.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-03.md:147:She did not say the word. Cael understood why, even then, standing in the circle with eleven seconds of silence still sitting in his chest. It was not unkindness. The word had already arrived and they both knew it, and saying it aloud would only have been for form, and she was not a person who did things for form.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-03.md:171:He opened his notebook to the page with the date and the hour, and under them he wrote *Eleven seconds*, and closed it again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:22:- **Cael:** nineteen (turned nineteen on the page in Book 7 Ch14, in autumn). **There is no birthday in this book.** He has **ten confirmed fragments**: Wind (Lira), Pressure ("the Bronze from Ardenmere" in spoken inventories), Iron (Brom), Compression (Reydan), Ember (Karis), Shadow (Seln; seal held), Storm (Daeva), Anchor (unnamed; used both ways), Blade (Ephram) and Tide (Oryn; partial; Iron; self and contact; clinical; *ask first, every time*). **The still place** is not a fragment and is not counted. It is the Book 2 anomaly converged, read three times, untouched since Oryn said "stop touching it". His Arbiter has been dark since his Kindling. His ribs have been quiet since the marsh. 96 marks in hand; nothing owed. Pike's sheet shows nine completed lines under the five names, and the Stair's price column reads *three hundred; taken; not paid*. The crew is **eleventh** on the wall and the season line is blank.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:32:- **Cael:** nineteen, with **thirteen confirmed fragments**. Iron Wall came from Vastin in the Treswick alley (entered, **not told**; the debt stands; deployment none). Rune came from Cleon in the chamber (told to Cleon; the warning stands). Force came from Teague at the Span's barricade (first and only use on stone at the pass; the recoil stops in the ribs; never used on a person). **The still place** has been read by two instruments and understood by neither. Tide reads nothing, with everything going round it. Rune reads a shape with bones, a floor with nothing built on it. At the court's centre there was **no edge**: "Not the Quiet. Not a fragment. The same kind of made." **He told no one the Rune finding.** His Arbiter is dark and unchanged. The instruments read wrong in his hands and only his, with the **trend up** (a hair at Lowmarch; a stride short and "a hair and a half, two" at the plain's edge). That fact is unsourced. 123 marks; nothing owed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:84:34. **KEEP — The Span (304th).** First exchange: the watch's declaration is unanswered; Brom takes bolts on Iron. Teague goes through the middle (the barricade a body's length back) → **Force acquired** at a stride. Recoil: wrist, then elbow. The watch reforms. **Width:** "Nothing was gained inside" is entered first. "By count": five over stake thirty-four (Cael, Lira, Karis, the young one, Vastin), four on the shelf. They walk to the pinch. Eleven seconds. "You'll die in there." / "We've been in three times." The watch goes east, unpursued. The win sentence (§10). The broad man goes over the sink's edge in the fourth second; Teague does not turn. Oryn sets and knits the leg and is grey for a quarter-hour. The healers' nod. Teague's arm: "That's a fact about a line." / "Noted" / "Everybody says that now." Brom's bill. **The Span** at noon: an arch on two piers over the sink, carvings on the piers' inner faces, the bearing "Through". Nothing happens on the crown. The thirteenth listens. The log (§10).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:266:| **The Arbiter** | Checked M9 | Dark since Kindling; nothing for the Quiet to take | Unchanged |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:323:- **The Span (M8).** A shelf a cart's width wide, with the sink's three-man drop on the left and the iron on the right; the line lies a stride past the iron. A barricade of two timbers stands at the shelf's narrowest; past the fire is a pinch a stride and a half wide, then a perimeter road a pace wide. There are seven defenders: two crossbows and an empty-handed low practitioner. Exchange one belongs to the watch. Exchange two: Teague's line, with Cael at his left a stride back. Exchange three: width (five inside two strides in, four on the shelf; walk; eleven seconds). Then the fall. **Fairness is entered before the act:** nothing is gained inside.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:407:- **S22 log body:** "Turned the Rune fragment on myself. Cleon said don't. The reading only ever told me the still place was nothing — no flow, no current, a hole where a man should have a middle. The Rune sense says it's not a hole. It says it's *built like something* — like one of those floors with nothing standing on it. Same shape to that fragment as the sites are to Cleon's. I'm not going to write what that might mean, because I don't know, and because the last time I wrote something true in this log a healer made me swear an oath about it. The instruments read wronger for me than they did at Lowmarch. The trend's climbing. My Arbiter's still dark—nothing in me to go quiet, same as always. Tomorrow, the oldest one. I've spent the whole year wanting to stand in the middle of it. I think part of me already knows what I'm going to feel. Nothing. I think I'm going to feel nothing, and I think that's going to be the loudest thing that's ever happened to me." ("the whole year", never "the whole book".)
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-56.md:53:He had not meant to. It was not in the plan, since nothing borrowed was in the plan, and he and Lira had settled that on the first morning of the straw post without either of them having to say it. But it had been sitting in him since Feryn's wall like a question at the end of a page: there were two of them now, side by side in the dark behind his breastbone, the leaning one and the heavy one, and he had only ever picked up one at a time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-40.md:265:It stayed. It did not go out, as the word had gone out. It sat there in the dark behind his breastbone, quiet and plain, like a figure on a page once it has been written down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-40.md:279:He sat on the cot for a long time with the notice there in the dark behind his breastbone, and did not write anything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-40.md:375:He blew out his small piece of the light by turning his face to the wall. He lay awake with the notice quiet behind his breastbone and his hip aching, and listened to the district's late sounds come up through the window.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-40.md:423:He had it with him, behind his breastbone, quiet and plain, in the dark place that was not quite as dark as it had been. *Wind-adjacent.* It had been there at Vell's table and on the wall and in the gate, and it was there now in the lane. Every step he took beside her, it was there, and every step he did not say it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-40.md:435:He did not know how long he could carry it, only that it would not be long. He thought, walking home with his hip aching, that it might be two weeks, if he was careful, and that he did not want to be careful, and that the thing behind his breastbone was partly hers. He thought that every morning he kept it from her in the grey half, it would get heavier. Nothing that has to be held is free.
