editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:13:| Cael | Nineteen (turned nineteen in Book 7 Ch14, in autumn). **No birthday this book.** Ribs quiet since the marsh, healed under a healer's two days that became four. Arbiter dark since the Kindling. The ten fragments are at rest. The still place is untouched since Oryn said "stop touching it". |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-04.md:37:The seal was dark wax pressed with the Compact's mark, a hand holding a balance, so cleanly that a machine must have done it. Cael thought, for no good reason, of the brass plate on Pellin's desk, and all that care poured into the outside of things. He broke the seal and unfolded the paper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-42.md:121:He told her about the quiet before a thought. He told her about the dark place behind his breastbone where the sigil had gathered at Kindling, and had been dark ever since, and how it had still been dark, and how something had been written in it anyway. He opened the Log to the back, to the page where he had copied it, and turned it round on his knee so that she could read it, and she leaned forward on the bed and read it with her lips moving very slightly, as she read the board.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/state/completion/listening-proof.md:512:| Suppression-Advisory Watch, Priority Level 4; Section 12 / Twelve; the supplementary marker (the green slip); the contact of record | numbers as words; "Level four" |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-52.md:305:He knew the way now, a little. He went into the dark place behind his breastbone and found the Wind there first, the leaning thing, the readiness, the shape the step had left. He went past it, carefully. Beyond it there was the other one, which he had felt sitting beside the first since the night after Feryn and had not touched. It did not lean. It was heavier than that, and lower, and quieter: a sort of weight in the dark that seemed to be waiting to be picked up, as a sack waits on a quay.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/state/movement-001/review-cold-prompt.md:1636:"Your first declaration comes at Kindling," he said. "Your Arbiter gives you your Path and your first ability together. After that, new declarations come as you climb. Not every rank brings one. Some do. You'll learn the rhythm of your own Path."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/state/movement-001/review-cold-prompt.md:1726:He had known since orientation that she was on the track. He had not, until that moment, thought about what that meant. The category had been built for someone. It had been built for her. A guild family's daughter who wanted to sit in lectures before her Kindling, so that when her Arbiter came she would already know the shape of the chart. That was the real use of the hollow circle. He was the one borrowing it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-40.md:209:*Kindling assessment, observer track. Oona. Registered Arbiter Station, town. Second bell.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-52.md:209:*Return, registry, to the senior evaluation seat. Origin of designation Suppression-Advisory Watch, Priority Level 4: Systemic protocol, registry sub-layer. Authorizing official: none of record. Issuing office: none of record. Determination: no originating authority of record; designation predates file creation.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-52.md:215:*Referral, senior evaluation seat, filed in the matter of the Greyvane adjudication: query as to originating authority for designation Suppression-Advisory Watch, Priority Level 4, appearing in the priority field of subject file. Determination: no originating authority of record. Designation predates file creation. No action available at this clearance. Administrative irregularity, referred.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/state/movement-002/review-cold-prompt.md:605:She did not use it. She did not have time. He was gone, a long clean step to her right, and her staff went through the air where he had been. But she had seen it, and she put it away somewhere behind her breastbone, carefully, the way Cael put a thing in the middle column. *He goes home first.* She would have that tomorrow. Whatever happened tonight, she would have that.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/state/movement-002/review-cold-prompt.md:1070:He thought about the word the registry had given him in a hall at fourteen, with the Arbiter gone dark over his head after eleven seconds. Somebody had done a sum about him too, in front of everybody, and written the answer down. He had never once thought of answering it. He had only been getting on with things, a page at a time, in three columns, in the dark. Now, sitting beside her on the step, he wondered whether that was the same thing she meant, done more quietly.
