editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/state/movement-004/pre-repair/chapter-28.md:19:He knew it at once; he had felt it four times in his life and every time something had been written afterward in the dark behind his breastbone: at Torvin's by a lamp, and twice more in his first year, and in Lira's alcove in the middle of a broom drill. There was no mistaking it. A hush began somewhere in the middle of him and moved out to the edges, and the morning went on without him, as if a heavy door had been swung shut on it from his side. The alcove went on. The lamp went on burning. Brom was a forearm away on his cross. But all of it was suddenly on the far side of something, very clear and very distant, like a room seen through a window at night.
