410:### Book 8 — as drafted (2026-09-05; 24 chapters closed; reconciled to the prose as B7's entry was)
   150	
   151	---
   152	
   153	## Book 5 — The Silver Standard
   154	
   155	**Promise:** Make the reader feel the tournament as the genuine proving ground it is — and make them sweat through every match.
   156	
   157	**External arc:** The Continental Academy Tournament — held every three years, open to all registered academies. Halcenvane qualifies. Cael cannot participate officially due to classification status. He participates anyway through a technicality Karis found: the tournament rules require that academies field any practitioner who appears on their enrollment record. His demonstration provision means he's on the record.
   158	
   159	**Internal arc:** Cael moves from *I can beat anyone at my tier* to *I am not at any tier.*
   160	
   161	**Tournament structure:** Each companion fights separately before the companions fight together. Each faces an opponent designed to test their specific arc: Lira vs. speed specialist who outpaces her, Brom vs. Glass Path striker who finds every gap in his defense, Karis vs. information-advantage opponent who knows her technique perfectly.
   162	
   163	**Main event:** Cael vs. a Gold-tier prodigy — Daeva, 19 years old, Gold Rank 3, the youngest Gold-tier practitioner in continental history. The match is the book's climax. Cael does not win cleanly. He survives long enough to demonstrate something that has never been recorded in a tournament: a practitioner integrating an opponent's ability mid-fight and immediately deploying it. The judges don't know how to score it.
   164	
   165	**Vastin escalation:** Vastin watches the Daeva match in person. After, he requests a private meeting with Cael. The meeting is civil. Vastin tells him: *You should stop competing publicly.* Cael asks why. Vastin says: *Because the people who want this to stop are not going to use official channels much longer.*
   166	
   167	This is the first honest warning Cael receives from an institutional source.
   168	
   169	**Ending:** Halcenvane places third in the tournament. Cael's match record stands as the most-reviewed result in tournament history. The Compact convenes a formal discussion about whether to classify [SHATTERED] as an active threat category.
   170	
   171	---
   172	
   173	## Book 6 — The Compact's Hand
   174	
   175	**Promise:** Make the reader feel the moment the institution drops the pretense — and make the companions' choice to walk out feel earned.
   176	
   177	**External arc:** The Compact moves from monitoring to direct action. They file a formal classification challenge that would remove Cael's demonstration-provision enrollment status and require him to submit to a Compact re-evaluation — which he would fail, because the Compact cannot evaluate [SHATTERED] architecture, and their procedure for failed evaluations is expulsion and asset-restriction.
   178	
   179	**Internal arc:** Cael moves from *I can work within the system* to *the system was never designed to include me.*
   180	
   181	**The revelation:** Seln provides his intelligence cache — the files he collected during his years as a Compact operative, which he has been carrying since he walked away from the job. The files confirm: the Guilds Compact has been falsifying Path classifications for two hundred years. Practitioners who develop abilities the Compact finds politically inconvenient are reclassified into lesser Paths at their Kindling evaluation. The falsification is built into the Arbiter evaluation protocol — a modification made so long ago that no current Compact official knows it's there.
   182	
   183	**Vastin's moment:** When the companions bring the intelligence cache to Vastin — who by this point has been receiving their trust in small increments — he is silent for a long time. Then: *I knew the system was imperfect. I didn't know it was designed to be.*
   184	
   185	He doesn't switch sides in this book. But he stops being the Compact's instrument.
   186	
   187	**The walk-out:** The companions leave Halcenvane together. Not expelled — they resign. Karis forfeits her ranking. Lira walks out of the last class she'll ever take at a formal institution. Brom carries three boxes of materials Karis has been compiling. Seln simply isn't at his desk the next morning.
   188	
   189	**Ending:** The companions are in the edge territories for the first time. They have no institutional backing, no ranking protection, and a Compact classification file that now reads: *active non-compliance, observation priority escalated.*
   190	
   191	Cael's fragment log now contains nine integrated abilities. He still doesn't have a tier.
   192	
   193	---
   194	
   195	# ARC 3 — THE WORLD
   196	**Books 7–9 | Ages 18–20 (drafted: B7 18→19 on the page, in autumn; B8 nineteen throughout — it runs winter's end to summer; B9 19→20 in autumn; the pre-drafting band read 20–22 — superseded) | Antagonist: The Quieting**
   197	
   198	The arc where the world gets larger and the true threat becomes visible.
   199	
   200	---
   201	
   202	## Book 7 — Void Roads
   203	
   204	**Promise:** Make the reader feel the edge territories as the first place Cael has ever been fully free — and then make that freedom complicated.
   205	
   206	**External arc:** The companions travel the edge territories, taking work as independent practitioners — monster clearance, route scouting, artifact recovery. They are good at this. They are, as a team, probably the most effective independent unit in the edge territories, because their combination of abilities (Wind/Iron Skin/Ember/Shadow/Fractured) covers every scenario and because four years of academy plus underground circuit experience has produced genuinely capable people.
   207	
   208	**Oryn joins.** Tide Path, Iron-tier, traveling healer who has been working the edge territories for two years. She finds them because a route scouting job goes wrong and she's the closest healer. She stays because she's never met anyone whose injuries she couldn't explain with standard Path mechanics, and Cael's injuries are consistently inexplicable.
   209	
   210	**The Quieting — first encounter:** An ancient ruin site where Path abilities stop working. The Arbiters go silent within 200 meters of the site perimeter. Oryn can't access her Tide Path healing. Cael, notably, is unaffected — his Fractured Path operates normally throughout. He doesn't mention this immediately. He needs to think about what it means.
   211	
   212	**Power development:** First integration of a non-combat ability — Oryn demonstrates a Tide Path diagnostic technique and Cael absorbs a fragment. He now has, in some partial form, the ability to perceive internal energy architecture. He uses it, quietly, to study his own.
   213	
   214	**Ending:** The companions locate three more Quieting sites. The sites are on an alignment — not random. Someone made this pattern.
   215	
   216	**As drafted (24 chapters closed 2026-09-04 — this block reconciles the entry above to the prose; the prose governs):**
   217	
   218	- **Roster:** FIVE cross the Line (Cael, Lira, Brom, Karis, Seln — Book 6's prose settled on five; "the sixth" was Seln's desk). Oryn walks with them from Thornwater as a stop on her route (Ch9), leaves after the Ring to make up her days (Ch16–17), and **joins on her own terms at Millrace (Ch18)** — a terms-of-service join, not a rescue; her route is intact and she is on it at book's end ("Find me at Thornwater." / "Bring the map." Ch23; "Six, by letter." Ch24).
   219	- **Calendar and age:** day 46 to day 130 past the Line. **Cael is eighteen at Ch1 and turns nineteen on the page (Ch14).** The Arc 3 band above (20–22) is now contradicted by drafted B6–B7 prose and Book 8 is being built on 19→20: **rebase the arc-wide age bands to the drafted timeline in the bible pass and mark the drafted timeline LOCKED; do not retrofit prose.**
   220	- **The Quieting — as staged:** four sites, not "one plus three": the Fallow Ring (Ch10–11), the Long Stair (Ch18–20), the Drowned Hall (Ch21–22), and a fourth sighted, paced from outside and NOT entered (Ch23) — twice the radius (400 m) and cordoned in Compact iron. Perimeter 200 m, sharp line, identical Arbiter failure, fused floors, carvings (52 sheets). Cael unaffected inside all three entered sites; five companions know; **the Compact does not.**
   221	- **Power development — as staged:** the Tide-adjacent fragment is acquired at the deep reading (Ch13), at Oryn's direction, cost written down in advance; the rule *ask first, every time*; he reads himself (Ch14, Ch22) and Oryn with consent (Ch22); the losable moral stake (reading Teague uninvited) is paid by not paying it. **The B2 Tide anomaly CONVERGES and is NOT resolved:** the tenth fragment locates a *still place* at the architecture's centre — not the Quieting, not a fragment, unexplained.
   222	- **The Compact reaches across the Line twice:** on paper (Ch8 — a recovery contract whose completion report told the poster which crew walks out of quiet ground) and in person (Ch17–19 — a nameless six on the Long Stair's rim with crossbows and an Anchor course, aimed at Seln's case and the boy; repelled; no faces; the Compact saw a declaration going out, nothing working inside). Vastin: one window, writing nothing (Ch16).
   223	- **Fragment count at close: ten.** Wind, Pressure, Iron, Compression, Ember, Shadow, Storm, Anchor, Blade, Tide. **Trajectory flag:** twenty-two by Book 10 leaves twelve for Books 8–9 — Book 8's architecture must plan an accelerated rate and say so.
   224	- **Ending — as drafted:** the alignment is proved on a carter's map with two brass straightedges (Ternhall's and Hesk's gift) at Lowmarch in first snow; "Someone made this pattern." is asked once and not answered. Teague's spring term: "Whatever's on that bearing. My crew comes."
   225	- **What Book 8 inherits:** the fourth site (400 m, cordoned, unentered — the on-ramp); the Compact survey kit and its field ledger (a radius line in the Compact's own hand; an index of forty-one kinds; the stroke-and-bar mark); the still place; the reading turned outward (re-armed stake); Oryn's route and Lira's arm waiting on her word; Teague in spring; the Compact's demonstrated awareness of quiet ground (and its ignorance of the inside); Cael nineteen, ten fragments; letters west (Hesk, Vell).
   226	- **What Book 8 must not pre-pay:** Vastin's departure and classified-history material (not hinted in B7); the Architect's name; any maker for the pattern; the strata limit's meaning ([UNBOUND] used once, Ch15, as a stratum label only).
   227	- **Exceptions logged against the cards:** EXC-B7-001 (twelve weeks, Ch8 log), -002 (Lira's arm two weeks old, Ch17), -003 (Millrace re-siting, Ch17–19/23), -004 (the arrested stake, Ch20), -005 (trials from the Hall's outer coping, Ch21).
   228	
   229	---
   230	
   231	## Book 8 — Before the Paths
   232	
   233	**Promise:** Make the reader feel the weight of history — the sense that the present is built on something that was deliberately buried.
   234	
   235	**External arc:** The companions pursue the Quieting's origin. This means research — old records, pre-Compact archives, practitioners who specialize in historical Path studies. Vastin, operating now as an independent agent with no Compact sanction, makes contact and shares what he found in the Compact's classified history section before he left: a reference to something called the Architect, and a note that the Arbiter evaluation protocol contains a sub-layer that no living Compact official has administrative access to.
   236	
   237	**Internal arc:** Cael moves from *I know the system was built to exclude me* to *the system was built specifically to prevent me.*
   238	
   239	**The Architect's name:** In a pre-Compact archive at a border city, Karis finds the first primary source reference to the Architect — not as a myth but as a historical individual who designed and deployed the Arbiter system. The record dates to roughly 400 years before the series present. The record is damaged; the Architect's name is not readable, but their intent is: *the system exists to prevent the reemergence of [UNBOUND] potential, which nearly destroyed the continent before the system's deployment.*
   240	
   241	**Plant:** A damaged secondary source mentions that the Architect did not die in the conventional sense. The language used is obscure. Karis marks the passage.
   242	
   243	**Vastin arc:** Vastin obtains the Compact's complete Quieting records — which he shouldn't have, because the Compact has been suppressing the Quieting data for six years. He gives them to the companions without editorial comment. His arc this book: the man who spent a career enforcing institutional authority learning to navigate without it.
   244	
   245	**Ending:** The companions locate a third-tier Quieting site — one where the phenomenon has been stable for centuries, not recent. Someone made this on purpose, a long time ago. Cael stands at the center of it and feels nothing. His Fractured Path doesn't register the Quieting at all. He understands, now, that this is not coincidence.
   246	
   247	---
   248	
   249	## Book 9 — What Was Lost
   250	
   251	**Promise:** Make the reader feel the weight of the full reveal — the history Cael never knew he was part of.
   252	
   253	**External arc:** The companions find a tomb in the deepest edge territory — an ancient sealed chamber belonging to the last [UNBOUND] / [SHATTERED] practitioner before the record goes silent. The tomb is not a monument. It is a message, preserved by someone who knew they weren't going to survive and wanted someone to eventually understand what happened to them.
   254	
   255	**Internal arc:** Cael moves from *the system was built to prevent me* to *I know what I am.*
   256	
   257	**The tomb's contents:** Written record of an [UNBOUND] practitioner's experience — their Kindling, their discovery of their abilities, their encounter with the early Compact's predecessor organization, their understanding that the Architect's system was not designed to help them. The record ends before the practitioner's death but after they understood it was coming. The final entry: *I am leaving this for the next one. There will be a next one. The system cannot suppress this permanently — only delay it.*
   258	
   259	**Full revelation of the Fractured Path's nature:** Cael reads the record and understands. The [UNBOUND] practitioner describes their ability in terms Cael recognizes: integration of witnessed abilities, fragment notices, the absence of tier recognition, the Quieting not affecting them. He is not a defect. He is not an anomaly. He is a recurrence.
   260	
   261	**The Compact fractures:** Internal Compact documents, obtained by Seln, show that senior Compact officials have begun disagreeing about the Quieting suppression. Some want to continue covering it up. Some believe the Quieting is about to become impossible to hide. The institution that has been the antagonist for three arcs is beginning to collapse from within.
   262	
   263	**Vastin's choice:** Vastin formally requests to travel with the companions. Not as an operative. As himself. The companions take a vote. It's 3-2, with Seln the deciding vote: yes.
   264	
   265	**Ending:** The Quieting expands visibly for the first time into a city's outer districts. The Compact issues a public statement attributing it to a natural phenomenon. Nobody in the affected district believes them. The world is about to change.
   266	
   267	**Mystery fully planted:** The Architect's preserved will — dormant in the Arbiter system since the system's creation — received a signal when Cael's Kindling registered [SHATTERED]. It has been in a slow-activation sequence for six years. The activation is nearly complete.
   268	
   269	---
   270	
   271	# ARC 4 — THE STORM
   272	**Books 10–12 | Ages 21–24 (rebased to the drafted timeline: one year per book from B9's 21; the pre-drafting band read 23–25) | Antagonist: The Architect's preserved will**
   273	
   274	The arc where everything Cael has built is tested at the cost of something real.
   275	
   276	---
   277	
   278	## Book 10 — The Quieting
   279	
   280	**Promise:** Make the reader feel the scale of a continent losing something it didn't know it could lose.
   281	
   282	**External arc:** The Quieting reaches three major city-states simultaneously. Path abilities in affected zones stop working entirely — not just weakening, not just disruption, but cessation. Practitioners who have relied on their abilities since Kindling experience sudden, total loss. The Guilds Compact's suppression attempt collapses when the Compact's own headquarters district goes Quiet.
   283	
   284	**Vastin arc:** Vastin testifies before a continental emergency council — the first such council in 200 years. He presents the Compact's suppressed Quieting records. This ends his career as a Compact official permanently and formally and starts his role as the companions' institutional voice with the surviving governing bodies.
   285	
   286	**Cael's position:** The companions are now the most knowledgeable group on the Quieting's origin and mechanism. This makes them both essential and extremely exposed.
   287	
   288	**Power development:** Cael, now 23, has integrated 22 abilities across multiple Path types. His functional capability is Gold-tier equivalent in combat, though the Arbiter system still reads him as [SHATTERED]. In Quieting zones, he is the only practitioner whose abilities function. This makes him, for the first time, not just unusual but singular.
   289	
   290	**Ending:** The Compact formally dissolves. Seven separate continental bodies claim jurisdictional authority. Three of them immediately begin competing over the response to the Quieting. The companions stop waiting for institutional solutions.
   291	
   292	---
   293	
   294	## Book 11 — The Architect's Will
   295	
   296	**Promise:** Make the reader feel the arrival of something that has been waiting — not malevolent, not mindless, but implacable.**
   297	
   298	**External arc:** The Architect's preserved will completes its activation sequence. Its first observable action: targeted suppression of Fractured Path signatures. Not the Quieting — something more specific. Equipment fails specifically when Cael uses it. Navigation records are corrupted around his location. Practitioners near him begin experiencing interference with their own abilities — not Quieting, but something that seems tied to his presence.
   299	
   300	**Internal arc:** Cael moves from *I know what I am* to *I need to know what the Architect is — and whether I can face it.*
   301	
   302	**The Architect's will — described:** It does not speak. It does not appear. It is the system itself, behaving with intention. The companions observe that every institutional mechanism in Valdris — Arbiter stations, Compact Registry fragments, city-access gates — is beginning to route its actions in ways that disadvantage a single practitioner. The Architect's will has no face. It is the shape of a decision made four centuries ago, executing.
   303	
   304	**Key scene:** Cael, alone at an Arbiter station, asks his Arbiter — the sigil that has been dark since his Kindling — to tell him what it sees when it evaluates him. The Arbiter activates, for the first time, and says: *I cannot evaluate you. You predate my categories.* Then it goes dark again. This is the first confirmation, from within the system itself, of what the tomb record described.
   305	
   306	**Ending:** The Architect's will issues its first direct action against Cael: a systemic command, propagated through every active Arbiter station on the continent, to revoke practitioner status from any entity whose Path architecture registers as [SHATTERED]. It is bureaucratic violence — suddenly Cael has no legal standing anywhere in Valdris that operates an Arbiter station. This does not stop him. It does make everything harder.
   307	
   308	---
   309	
   310	## Book 12 — The Weight of Paths
   311	
   312	**Promise:** Make the reader feel the cost — not abstract, not distant, but something specific and irreplaceable.
   313	
   314	**External arc:** The companions operate under the systemic revocation from Book 11. The Architect's will has begun directly affecting the Quieting expansion — accelerating it, steering it. The companions piece together the mechanism: the Architect's reset protocol is not just suppressing the Quieting areas. It is actively deconstructing the Path architecture in those zones, preparing to rebuild from the system's original specification. A reset will return every practitioner's abilities to Kindling-level. It will eliminate every advancement made in four centuries.
   315	
   316	**Internal arc:** Cael moves from *I need to face this* to *I need to decide whether I'm willing to pay what facing it costs.*
   317	
   318	**The loss:** One companion — Seln — makes a choice that Cael cannot follow. Not a betrayal. A different decision about what the right path is. Seln believes the Architect's reset, while devastating, may be necessary — that the existing system, built on falsification and suppression, cannot be reformed, only replaced. He is not wrong about the system's corruption. He is not right that the reset is the answer. He leaves the companions to pursue his own response to the Quieting. He is still alive. He is no longer here.
   319	
   320	This is the most honest disagreement the series has generated between members of the found family. It is not resolved in this book.
   321	
   322	**Cael's sacrifice:** To prevent the reset from reaching a major population center in time to warn people, Cael permanently surrenders three integrated abilities — abilities he has carried for years, that have become native to how he fights and thinks. The sacrifice works. The reset is delayed. The cost is visible in every scene afterward: he moves differently without them, reaches for things that aren't there.
   323	
   324	**Ending:** The companions, minus Seln, arrive at the location where the Architect's preserved will is most concentrated — an ancient site that predates every other structure in Valdris. This is where the confrontation happens. Cael, for the first time, feels the Architect's presence as something he can approach rather than something operating on him. He is not ready yet. But he is close.
   325	
   326	---
   327	
   328	# ARC 5 — THE RECKONING
   329	**Books 13–15 | Ages 24–27 (rebased; the pre-drafting band read 26–28) | Antagonist: The Architect**
