editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-52.md:305:He knew the way now, a little. He went into the dark place behind his breastbone and found the Wind there first, the leaning thing, the readiness, the shape the step had left. He went past it, carefully. Beyond it there was the other one, which he had felt sitting beside the first since the night after Feryn and had not touched. It did not lean. It was heavier than that, and lower, and quieter: a sort of weight in the dark that seemed to be waiting to be picked up, as a sack waits on a quay.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-18.md:305:She had never asked him. You did not ask; declarations were private, and an Arbiter was the most private thing a person had, and she had not asked anybody what their Arbiter said since the week of her own Kindling.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-49.md:101:"Section one also says what a primary classification is. *The classification returned by the practitioner's Arbiter at Kindling and entered in the registry, including any entry made in the Supplementary Register.*" He did not hurry. "Mine was returned at Kindling. It took eleven seconds. It's entered in the Supplementary Register, statute nineteen, with an incidence count. I have a primary classification. It isn't one the standard framework can use, which is why section four's first half fits me. But section four lets you enter a practitioner into the category, and section one says who the category is for. It's for practitioners without one. Not being assessable by ordinary means isn't the same as not being classified."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-03.md:1:# Chapter 3 — Eleven Seconds
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-03.md:67:Pellin found the file, checked a line with her pen, and made a small mark. "Kindling assessment, the usual way. Your Arbiter wakes here, and the result goes straight to the registry ledger." She laid two fingers on a flat brass plate let into the desktop at her elbow, dull and unremarkable, about the size of a book. "When your result reaches the Registry, this lights, and I record what it shows. The resolution itself is usually done before you've finished a breath; with the paperwork, the whole appointment runs from three minutes to fifteen. Do you have questions first?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-03.md:81:A year of being ready, and he was not ready, and it turned out you could be both. He knew the procedural summary by heart: *The practitioner stands within the marked circle. No external device is required. Every practitioner's Arbiter is their own, dormant since birth, and Kindling is the moment it wakes.* What the waking felt like, nobody had managed to tell him; only that it came.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-03.md:87:It did not come from outside him; that was the first thing, and none of the eleven descriptions on page seven had told him so. He had imagined something arriving, as light arrives through a window; instead it gathered from inside him, from somewhere behind his breastbone, as though it had always been there and had only now decided to be noticed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-03.md:91:He glanced at Pellin, who was three feet away watching the brass plate, and her face showed nothing, because she could not see it and nobody could; it was his, and it had always been his, and nobody else would ever see it. It was his Arbiter.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-03.md:101:By now, he understood, the plate should have lit; Pellin had said it was usually done before you finished a breath, and he had finished one. He kept her face at the edge of his eye; her face was information. She was not looking at him but at the brass plate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-03.md:127:Eleven seconds. He was sure of the number. He had counted every one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-03.md:135:The brass plate at Pellin's elbow lit, eleven seconds late. Text rose up in it, pale and even, and settled into the same word he had just heard.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-03.md:147:She did not say the word. Cael understood why, even then, standing in the circle with eleven seconds of silence still sitting in his chest. It was not unkindness. The word had already arrived and they both knew it, and saying it aloud would only have been for form, and she was not a person who did things for form.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-03.md:171:He opened his notebook to the page with the date and the hour, and under them he wrote *Eleven seconds*, and closed it again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-42.md:121:He told her about the quiet before a thought. He told her about the dark place behind his breastbone where the sigil had gathered at Kindling, and had been dark ever since, and how it had still been dark, and how something had been written in it anyway. He opened the Log to the back, to the page where he had copied it, and turned it round on his knee so that she could read it, and she leaned forward on the bed and read it with her lips moving very slightly, as she read the board.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-06.md:61:He had always thought of the watching as a kind of hiding: a way of standing a half-step outside things so they could not quite get hold of him, and something he would have to grow out of sooner or later. In the circle he had counted eleven seconds. On the morning of the notice he had named what his own hands were doing so that he would not have to feel it. In the archive he had copied the entry out in his best writing so that he would not have to just sit there and know it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-58.md:43:He had not asked for it. There was no time to ask for anything, and he had not asked for it since the trough. It came up out of the dark behind his breastbone as it had come in the sleet, already done before he knew it was happening, and the yard was a hand's width narrower than it had been. His weight was not where his heel had been. It was round the edge of the blow, on Darrow's left, at an angle no step of his own could have got to from where he stood. The straight went through the space where his chest had been, close enough that he felt it go by along his shirt.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-58.md:105:He would go back over it a great many times, on the cot, and in the grey half, and on the wall by the pump, and it never once came out with a choice in it. His weight was going round on the Wind, and it went further than the Wind went. And in the dark behind his breastbone, beside the leaning thing, the heavy one was there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-58.md:233:Cael felt the quiet all round him, and three hundred people inside it waiting to carry something home. He thought of the Log in his coat on the bench, open at last night's page. He thought of the line under *Evidence* with nothing after it but *Ruling*. He had filled that line in this afternoon, for the crossing. There was another line, under all of it, that had never had anything written beside it at all, since a word on a brass plate in Denvash, and there was nothing beside it now. Something in him had done a thing he could not describe. He had watched it happen from the inside, like a tenant watching the house he rents get up and walk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-58.md:321:It came as the others had come. There was the quiet first, the quiet before a thought arrives whole; then the turning over; then, in the dark place behind his breastbone where nothing had lit since the word, something written.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-04.md:37:The seal was dark wax pressed with the Compact's mark, a hand holding a balance, so cleanly that a machine must have done it. Cael thought, for no good reason, of the brass plate on Pellin's desk, and all that care poured into the outside of things. He broke the seal and unfolded the paper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-09.md:107:Cael thought about Pellin's office, the circle of pale wood, the brass plate that lit eleven seconds late. He thought about a form drawn from the back of a drawer, filled in with small quick strokes, and how nobody in that room had asked him anything, because there had not been anything to ask; the question had already been answered by someone who was not in the room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-20.md:49:"It came the way everybody says it does, at Weaver's Row. From behind here." He laid two fingers on his breastbone. "It sat at the side of my eye for eleven seconds. Then it gave me one word and went out, like a lamp when you pinch the wick, and it hasn't come back." The next part was harder, and he made himself go on to it, because he had not walked all this way to stop at the easy half. "I didn't try it again for four weeks. I was frightened of what I'd find. Last night I went down into Torvin's yard at midnight and tried it properly, over and over. I don't know how many times. I stopped counting."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-20.md:267:None of it had gone: the absence sat behind his breastbone exactly where it had sat at midnight, and the three things were still in their corner, one of them counted. The review was still on a shelf in Denvash, propped against a clock, and somewhere a polite man with boots from somewhere else still knew there was a circuit kid at Torvin's.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-19.md:225:He called up the circle in Pellin's office, the pale wood worn smooth in two places side by side. He called up the feeling of putting his feet where the wood was worn, and the thing that had gathered behind his breastbone, small, and risen, and come to rest at the edge of his sight, just off to one side. *Every practitioner's Arbiter is their own, dormant since birth.* The procedural summary had said that you reached it by attending to the place behind the breastbone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-19.md:229:He attended to the place behind his breastbone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-19.md:245:He opened his eyes and looked to the edge of his sight, left, where it had hung, steady as a nail, for eleven seconds. Then right. Then left again, quickly, as you turn your head for a sound you are sure you heard.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-19.md:249:He tried again, and again, and he counted, because he had always counted, though he would have given a good deal not to. Four. Five. Each reach he held for longer than the one before, well beyond what was sensible, as a swimmer stays under after his chest has begun to ache, only to learn where the bottom of his breath is, and all the while the dark behind his breastbone lay there and let him. Six. Seven.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-19.md:253:And it was not silence. He knew that now, standing in the yard, as clearly as he had known it in the side room. Silence would have meant something there, choosing to keep quiet, as Pellin's brass plate had kept dark for eleven seconds before it lit. He could have waited out a silence. He could have knocked louder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-19.md:273:Every one of them could reach into the dark behind their breastbone and find a light there, and the light would tell them what they were. Not the whole of a person, but the part the city cared about, the part that decided which side of the bridge you slept on: tier, rank, Path, whether you were getting better.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-41.md:17:He had been putting off this page for three days. Every night he had opened the back section and looked at the white paper under the last entry, and closed it again, because what he wanted to write was not a fact. Everything else in the back of the book was a fact. The cart was a fact; the drop was a fact; the notice, with its plain words in the dark behind his breastbone, was the plainest fact of all. What he wanted to write tonight was a guess, and he had told Lira once, on a step under a lantern, that guessing was how you wrote the wrong thing down and then trusted it because it was written.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-41.md:33:The wheezing man had begun his long whistle by the door. Yeni's lamp was down to a bead, and when he looked up she had turned on her side with her face to the wall, which meant either that she was asleep or that she had decided to let him think so. He put the Log back on the shelf beside Hesk's leather book and lay down on his right side, the side that did not catch, and lay awake with the hip aching and the dark place behind his breastbone quiet and a little less dark than it had been.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-41.md:99:If it was anybody, and not only Lira, then it was everybody he had ever watched hard enough. Renn's figure, Dessa's patience, the kiln boy's held breath, Sarel's closing, Talis planting where he could not see him go, Corbin reading feet at the mark: any of them. The woman with the shoulder, too, doing her six positions in the old man's yard every evening for three months while he walked past. Any of them might have left something behind in him, quietly, as the lock had left its shape in the key, and it might be sitting there now in the dark behind his breastbone with no notice on it yet, waiting.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-41.md:153:He went to the dark place behind his breastbone, which was not quite as dark as it had been, and found the Wind there. It was not a word or a picture, but a sort of leaning in the dark, the shape the step had left when it went through him. He did not reach for it. He went past it, carefully, as you go past a sleeping dog, and felt about in the dark beyond it for anything that was not Lira's. He put the whole morning in front of himself, the heels settling and the sack going up a half beat late and the tar on the big hands, and reached for whatever it had left.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-48.md:69:He had copied the three lines letter by letter from the back of the Log, and checked them twice against the page, and then once more against the place in the dark behind his breastbone where they had been written first.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-48.md:269:He read them one at a time, with his finger under the line. *Assessor.* No. *Senior evaluation.* No. *Primary classification: the classification returned by the practitioner's Arbiter at Kindling and entered in the registry, including any entry made in the Supplementary Register.* He stopped. He read it again, and his finger did not move.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-48.md:281:The category was not for anybody whose classification could not be read. It was for people who did not have one. Section four gave an assessor the power to put a practitioner into the category, and section one, three pages earlier, said who could be in it. A boy whose Arbiter had returned nothing would be in it. A boy whose return had been lost, or never recorded, would be in it. And a boy whose Arbiter had taken eleven seconds and then returned a word, a real word, a word with four instances and a statute of its own in the Supplementary Register, and a brass plate that had lit eleven seconds late, had a primary classification. He had more of one than anybody in the district. It simply was not one the standard framework could use.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-48.md:291:*Three. Section one also says what a primary classification is: what the Arbiter returned, entered in the registry, including the Supplementary Register. Mine was entered there. Statute nineteen. Four instances. It resolved; the plate lit.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-49.md:95:It never did, anywhere. Every room she had slept in since she was sixteen had a single pane of thick leaded glass, with a small brass plate on the sill to say what it was rated for, and the rating was always her own assessed ceiling and a margin. The program's facilities office ordered them. She had stopped trying latches years ago.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-47.md:83:It came as the first one had, thirty-seven nights before, on this same cot. There was no sound and no light, and nowhere it could be said to come from. There was the quiet first, the hush in which a whole thought gets ready to arrive, and then a turning over, and then, in the dark place behind his breastbone where nothing had lit since the word, something written.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-47.md:169:He shut the book and set it on the shelf beside Hesk's leather one. He lay down very carefully on his back, which was the only way left that ought not to hurt, and it hurt anyway. He lay looking up at the beam with its old nail holes in the light of Yeni's lamp. Behind his breastbone, side by side in the dark, sat the two things, the one that leaned and the one that pressed, and he listened to the wheezing man begin to whistle.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-47.md:283:He thought about two lines of figures in the dark behind his breastbone where there had been one. There were two doors, perhaps, where he had thought there was only one, and he did not know where either of them went, or which of them he had come through, or whether there were others in the dark he had not found yet. He did not know what Kestrel had been, or what Feryn's hand had been, or whether Lira's three months and Feryn's one second were the same thing at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-40.md:265:It stayed. It did not go out, as the word had gone out. It sat there in the dark behind his breastbone, quiet and plain, like a figure on a page once it has been written down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-40.md:279:He sat on the cot for a long time with the notice there in the dark behind his breastbone, and did not write anything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-40.md:375:He blew out his small piece of the light by turning his face to the wall. He lay awake with the notice quiet behind his breastbone and his hip aching, and listened to the district's late sounds come up through the window.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-40.md:423:He had it with him, behind his breastbone, quiet and plain, in the dark place that was not quite as dark as it had been. *Wind-adjacent.* It had been there at Vell's table and on the wall and in the gate, and it was there now in the lane. Every step he took beside her, it was there, and every step he did not say it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-40.md:435:He did not know how long he could carry it, only that it would not be long. He thought, walking home with his hip aching, that it might be two weeks, if he was careful, and that he did not want to be careful, and that the thing behind his breastbone was partly hers. He thought that every morning he kept it from her in the grey half, it would get heavier. Nothing that has to be held is free.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-28.md:21:He knew it at once; he had felt it four times in his life and every time something had been written afterward in the dark behind his breastbone: at Torvin's by a lamp, and twice more in his first year, and in Lira's alcove in the middle of a broom drill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-12.md:127:"The Arbiter took about a minute and a half." Her eyes were on the wall's dark bulk, not on him. "It came, and I saw it, and it looked at me, and then the station clerk wrote *Copper* on a sheet and *Rank One*, and that was me. A minute and a half. Two years now I've been trying to get somebody to look longer than that." She was quiet a moment. "That man tomorrow has a room built for nothing but looking. A dozen instruments. He's got a clerk to time your blinks."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-39.md:11:Brom walked out across the grey stone to the table. His forearms were still wound in linen from wrist to elbow, and he carried them a little out from his sides. The clerk lifted the plaque off the grey cloth and gave it to him. It was a plain board of dark wood with a brass plate let into it, the tournament's bar-and-ring cut at the top and a line of small type underneath.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-43.md:23:*The fifth came two weeks ago, three nights before the six weeks ran out, while I sat on my cot at Torvin's. Something came up where the Arbiter used to be, written in the dark. I'll copy it exactly, because you'd want it exact.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-56.md:53:He had not meant to. It was not in the plan, since nothing borrowed was in the plan, and he and Lira had settled that on the first morning of the straw post without either of them having to say it. But it had been sitting in him since Feryn's wall like a question at the end of a page: there were two of them now, side by side in the dark behind his breastbone, the leaning one and the heavy one, and he had only ever picked up one at a time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:119:Cael counted it. He could not help counting it. From the moment the pen touched the first column to the moment it lifted from the second, eleven seconds.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:121:He filed them where the other eleven lived. A circle of pale wood in an office at Denvash, on the morning of his Kindling, and a brass plate at Pellin's elbow that had stayed dark for eleven seconds before it lit, while a fourteen-year-old boy stood in the circle and did not breathe. He had thought then that eleven seconds was the longest any office would ever take to decide what he was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-32.md:55:"Through the door. With a key or something as good as one." She tapped the end of her drawn room. "The inner door has a counting lock. The Compact makes every chartered records office on the continent fit one. Each time the bolt goes across, it knocks a little brass plate inside the case, and the registrar reads the plate. Bracken reads his every Fifth-day, never oftener, because a registrar seen reading his lock plate on an odd day is a registrar who's telling the building he expects a thief. He read it yesterday anyway. Two marks. This morning, four." She held up four fingers, then folded two of them down, then the other two. "In and out. In and out. Two nights."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-22.md:181:Since his Kindling he had not had one day without somebody writing him down. Assessors, a panel, an Arbiter with a classification, a warden with a challenge in his pocket, and a semester evaluation waiting at the end of this one; grey coats at every gate and ferry on two roads; and now a whole hill that had bought his transcript at six silver marks a copy and read it before he walked through the gate. He had grown so used to it that he only noticed it now in the way a man notices his own coat when somebody else asks him to take it off.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-20.md:1:# Chapter 20 — Eleven Seconds
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-20.md:103:Eleven seconds. Cael had counted them in his own breath without meaning to, from the table's first word to its last, the walks back to chalk left out. Three exchanges. Four bursts. And his own hip, forty feet up and three years away from where it had first learned this, ached with each of them in turn. It knew the whole bill: toll at the front, jolt at the back, and the hot line from crest to inner thigh that would be written out on her by suppertime and still legible at breakfast. She had known that price at breakfast. She had read Nyle's page over his shoulder and decided, between two mouthfuls of bread, what the bout was worth to her, and she had spent precisely that and kept the rest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-20.md:111:It was not the hush of people who have been impressed. Impressed was loud, a roar and a stamping. Cael had heard this other quiet twice before, both times in the Ironyard, both times on nights when something happened on the boards that the crowd had no place in its head to put. Every person in that yard had been handed the sheet that morning and had believed it, as you believe a printed timetable. They had trusted that ninety-one names had been put in their right order by people who knew. And now, in eleven seconds, in front of all of them, the sheet had been caught out about one name, beyond any argument, and every one of them knew it at once, and not one of them could have said what it was they knew.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-07.md:139:There was a case of glass on a stone plinth along one wall, and in it three crowns on three dark cushions with a brass plate under each. The Copper was the plainest of them, and the darkest, and worn bright along its top edge where a great many hands had lifted it. Its plate had been lengthened twice with new brass riveted on below the old.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:123:The hood received it. The clerk laid the sheet on the pile of things received and reached for the next. Eleven seconds.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:59:"Advisory, the same word, on a designation nobody at any height has ever been able to trace to an office." Karis drew a breath. "Cael told me on the third night, when he brought the bread. I asked him about Denvash, and he told me: eleven seconds. The plate lit eleven seconds late, and the word came at the end of them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:63:"Eleven seconds is a long time for a reading," said Karis. "Every other reading on the continent is done before you've finished a breath. I think the function had his reading in front of it for eleven seconds and couldn't find a column to put it in, any column, and couldn't take the margin off because it was all margin. So it did what a good form does when it can't be filled in: it failed safe, and wrote the one word it had for a thing that couldn't be written. Then it marked the file, so that whoever came after would know there was something there it hadn't been able to correct." She laid the slip down. "The mark has no office behind it because no office made it. It made itself, the way a stamp comes down when a lever's pulled. And Ilsev sent it up, at the hearing, twice, to every height she could reach, and asked who'd authorized it. Something answered her each time, something that wasn't anybody."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-21.md:37:He felt it want to, quite clearly, as though somebody had put a hand flat on his chest. A small cold current went up from his stomach through the middle of him and sat under his breastbone, and he knew the feeling, because he had last had it standing in a circle of pale wood on Weaver's Row while a brass plate failed to light.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-48.md:107:Eight was cleaner than seven; he had expected it to be worse. Nine went through him at the right angle and still left the place behind his breastbone ringing like a struck pan.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:49:*Every person who has ever decided a thing about me had a face, and I could read it. Pellin at Denvash, with her brass plate. Coss across a desk. Vastin with his frames. The provost who was frightened and couldn't hide it. Umber polishing his spectacles over a sheet he couldn't score. I read every one of them from across a room, and every reading was worth something.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md:147:It was the leaf about his Kindling. He had not known Karis had written one. It set out, in her plainest prose, the morning at Weaver's Row in Denvash: the circle of pale wood, the registrar who conducted it, the brass plate that lit late, the one bracketed word on the card, the classification line stamped where it had stood empty for fourteen years. Every fact in it was true and every fact was his, and he had never heard any of it in another person's sentences before. He read it in his own voice to a registrar in the middle of the night with the rain on the roof, and Bracken's finger moved down the other copy a line behind him, and twice Bracken said "Slower," and once he said "Again, that line," and made a small mark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:119:He thought of Denvash, which he did not often let himself do. A district office on a cold morning, a chalk circle on boards worn into two shallow places where a generation of feet had stood, and a warden-adjunct with tired grey eyes three feet away at a desk with a brass plate let into it. Something had woken behind his breastbone, small, a point of light at the edge of his sight that he could feel and never point to. He had counted eleven seconds while the plate did not light, and then the word had come, very far away and very close, and the plate lighting late with the same word in it, and the light at the edge of his sight going out like a pinched wick.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:121:All his life since, he had said that his Arbiter had spoken once and gone dark. He had said it to panels and wardens and Magisters, and written it in two books. It had been the one fixed fact at the bottom of every argument he had ever won. It had read him once, and said one word, and the registry had written down that word because it had nothing else to write, and he lived in the gap the word left.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:123:The leaf said there were two things in that office, not one. There was the reading, and there was something appended to it. And there had been eleven seconds.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:91:*Four streets back from the seat, on the wharf side. The tall house with the brass plate. The third floor. The second bell after noon. Neither office's. Nothing written.*
