editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-43.md:149:On the corner of the fish market there was a public house with two doors. The front one was propped open on a room full of wharfmen and smoke. The other, round the side, was shut, with a small brass plate screwed to it at the height of a man's eyes. Cael stopped and read the plate. It said that the back room was kept for ranked custom, and it gave the rank in a form of words that the registry had stopped using before Cael was born. Nobody had changed the plate. Nobody had needed to. Everybody who used that door knew what it meant.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-22.md:29:"Denvash," he had said, and stopped, because that was enough. Every one of them knew the brass plate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:15:He told the circle chalked on the worn boards of a district office, and the warden-adjunct three feet away watching the brass plate let into her desk, and the thing that woke behind his breastbone and came up to the edge of his sight. He told the eleven seconds he had counted while the plate did not light, and the one word that came at the end of them, very far away and very close, and the light at the edge of his sight going out like a pinched wick and never coming back when he looked. He told the letter that had made Denvash impossible for Hesk's sake, and the road east. He told Lira next, because Lira had come next: six weeks of bouts in a yard at Ardenmere, with her knocking him down on the boards between them more times than either of them had counted. Then one cold night on a cot under a window, with the Log on his knee, something had been written in the dark place behind his breastbone where the light had gone out.
