editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-12.md:217:He thought about the word the registry had given him in a hall at fourteen, with the Arbiter gone dark over his head after eleven seconds. Somebody had done a sum about him too, in front of everybody, and written the answer down, and he had never once thought of answering it. He had only been getting on with things, a page at a time, in three columns, in the dark. Now, sitting beside her on the step, he wondered whether that was the same thing she meant, done more quietly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-42.md:73:Lira saw it too, a step later than he did. She stopped. She stood in a narrowing strip of honest wood with Fiske's slick on both sides of her and across her front, and every inch of the strip had been drawn, in eleven seconds, by a woman who knew where Lira's feet liked to go and had simply removed all of those places before Lira got there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md:5:It was not a bout anybody would remember. The name four places above her belonged to a Stone fifth-year who had stood near the top of the Copper sheet for two seasons by being very hard to move. He was not hard to move for long. Lira took him in three exchanges and three bursts, each touch arriving from a side his weight had just left, and the yard, which a fortnight ago had gone silent at eleven seconds, clapped politely and went back to its bread and its arguments. That was a measurement too, and Cael wrote it down: a thing that had been impossible on the second session had become, by the tenth, merely expected.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-27.md:21:Each post was meant to be that something. Bolted to every plinth, in letters a quarter of an inch high, was a brass plate saying what happened if they ever failed to be.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-27.md:113:One second-year was off toward the warden's shed before the instructor had finished the word *warden*. The porter, sent to the wing for the duty clerk, went with his broom still on his shoulder. She moved everybody back out of the north bay with a single flat word, and nobody touched the post. One of the second-years, coming back, stood in front of it and read the brass plate aloud, every word, in a voice that was not steady, as though reading it might still help. Nobody had the heart to stop her.
