editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-01.md:208:Cael knew it too. Declarations were the language an Arbiter used, and every practitioner received them from Kindling on: a small glowing sigil only you could see, and then words in a form only you could read, telling you what your Path had just given you at your new rank. They were the private record of a person's growth, written in a hand nobody else ever saw. People did share them, brothers sometimes, or married couples, or a teacher and a student who had trusted each other for years. But you did not ask, any more than you would ask to read someone's letters, and you did not show unless showing meant something. Lira knew all of this perfectly well. She asked because she liked watching Brom refuse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-05.md:131:He took her hand and came up. He was beginning to feel it now, the thing the bursts cost that was not in the hip or the breath: a hollowness behind his breastbone, as though somebody had scooped out a cupful of whatever was in there and not put anything back. Seven bursts in less than an hour. He had never spent seven in an hour in his life.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-05.md:175:"I can feel it." He could. The hollow behind his breastbone had widened, and it had a weight to it now, which made no sense, since it was an emptiness; it sat in his chest like a cold stone made of nothing. His hip throbbed with his heart. His breath kept wanting to stop on its own, halfway in. It had got the habit. "I said ten."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-05.md:237:He woke before light with the hollow behind his breastbone grown into something he could almost hold, a cold weight of nothing that sat on his chest like a cat. His breath kept stopping by itself halfway in. His left hip had set in the night into a single long line of ache from the crest of the bone to the inside of the knee. He went down the stairs one at a time with his hand on the wall. The collarbone had a mark on it the shape of a staff end. The shoulder would not lift above the level of his ear.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-05.md:245:He had not known, until that day, how much of him was the bursts. He had thought of them as a thing he did with his hip, as a man thinks of whistling as a thing he does with his mouth. But the hollow behind his breastbone did not feel like the hip, or the breath. It felt like the place he went to when the floor went short, the dark place behind the bone where the notices came. He had gone to it too often in too short a time and worn a hole in the floor of it. By the middle of the afternoon he could not have called the burst if his life had hung on it. He knew, without trying, that it was not there to call. It would come back. He was nearly sure it would come back. He lay on his bed and was nearly sure for several hours.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-45.md:69:The code had never held it. Only the Arbiter's instrument did: it wrote the word out on the morning of a Kindling, a flag of its own making. Generation after generation of enforcement had simply treated that flag as if it were a classification, because it looked like one and sat where one sat on the form, and because nobody in all that time, so far as he could tell, had ever thought to go and look.
