    63	Then he went through it again, more slowly, in case some subsection or appendix had got past him, or some cross-reference hidden in the margin of an amendment. After that he made a list of every place in the code where a category could lawfully be kept. There was the main enumeration, and the provisional annex, and the appendix of disputed standings. There were the amendment schedules, one for every revision, and the definitions cross-index at the back of the charter. He went through them one at a time, as he would go through every answer an opponent might have, and crossed each off, until nothing was left to guess at, only what remained. It had not got past him.
    64	
    65	On the sixth night he read the whole schedule a third time, by lamplight, from the first page to the last, with Prynn at the high desk silently refilling his cup at an hour when no duty on earth required her to still be in the building. The third reading said what the second had. Nowhere in the schedule, in all its generations of piled-up text, did the word appear. Not once. No footnote, no entry struck out and kept in the record of its own repeal, and not as something once proposed and voted down. The schedule kept its refusals as well. He had come across three: categories somebody had argued for and lost, each refusal written up with the same clerical care as everything else.
    66	
    67	His word was not even among the rejected ones.
    68	
    69	The code had never held it. Only the Arbiter's instrument did: it wrote the word out on the morning of a Kindling, a flag of its own making. Generation after generation of enforcement had simply treated that flag as if it were a classification, because it looked like one and sat where one sat on the form, and because nobody in all that time, so far as he could tell, had ever thought to go and look.
    70	
    71	For a long while he sat still with it and did not write anything.
    72	
    73	He did not trust it, because a thing that size never deserved trust the first time you saw it, and he had lost his worst bouts by throwing himself at a seam before he had made sure it was really there. There had to be a page he had missed, in this building or in a records hall three districts away: an amendment, an annex, an order somewhere that made the flag lawful. Nothing ran for centuries on nothing.
    74	
    75	But things did, and he had seen them do it. The Ardenmere circuit had run for generations on rules nobody had ever written into any charter. A market interview had been held over him on an authority that nobody in the square could have named. And at fourteen his own life had been turned off its road by one morning's procedure that everybody in the room called law, because everybody in every room always had. Custom in law's coat was nothing strange where he came from; so far as he could see, most of the world wore it.
    76	
    77	He wrote it down.
    78	
    79	---
    80	
    81	His tea had gone cold, and he did not notice until Prynn was standing at his elbow with the pot, looking at the full cup, and then at him, and then, because she could not help it, at the page.
    82	

