editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:121:"You know the margin I copied at Greyvane. The one in the old records, where an assessor once looked at somebody whose work went across many families, and couldn't fit him in any column, and wrote him down by what he could show instead, by judgment, with the old word." She did not drop her eyes. "I said it once, at Halcenvane, and closed it as a question. I'll say it once more, here, because I have to, and then not again. *Unbound.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:161:"I went through everything for the anomaly," she said to Cael, "for session nine. You asked me to, at Greyvane, and I've never forgotten that you asked." She did not look at the slip. "Anything in the forty that read across a family with nobody of that family near. Anything in the reports about a practitioner who took something from no one. Any memorandum that mentions Tide by name, or a reading at a distance, or one that came without an engagement. Anything in the leaf." She shook her head. "Nothing. Not one line in it touches session nine. The function corrects what the reading shows at Kindling. Your session nine wasn't a Kindling, and the reading didn't show it to anybody, and nobody corrected it. Whatever it is, the case didn't do it and doesn't know about it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:37:"And under all of them," he said, "there's one I don't count. Session nine, at fifteen: a piece of something like Tide, from nobody. There was no Tide practitioner within a day's ride. There was nothing at stake and nobody against me, nothing the rule says it needs, and it came anyway." He moved his hand on the cloth. "I don't know what it is. I've kept it out of the count for three years, because the day I count it I've decided something I don't know. I've got three words for it. *Still open. Still real. Patience.*"

