books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-08.md:71:Broker's paper, the thin kind. A clerk's hand: even, schooled, the letters sitting on a line that had not been drawn. In the corner, pressed into a drop of dark wax, the broker's seal — a hook and a bar — which he had seen in ink on a third of the bales on the landing, because they were that broker's bales. *Recovery,* it said. *Surveyor's instruments abandoned at a site on the Fallow road.* Then the site: *east of Thornwater; the second day; where the road leaves its line at the rise and comes back to it.* Then the price, written twice, as brokers wrote it, once in figures and once in words. A hundred marks. *One hundred.* Then the seal, and nothing else — no crew's mark on it, no HOLD, no name beside a word, nothing to say who had taken it, because nobody had.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-07.md:191:He took it apart, because that was what he did with a sentence that had been aimed. It was not unkind; he listened for unkind and it was not in it anywhere. It was a claim, with the evidence in it, and a ruling: two jobs, five people on each; one done well; one that had cost a cart. Every word was true, and the ruling was the one he would have written himself over the traverse if he had been Pike, or Vell, or a boy in a boarding house with a notebook and a circuit to price. He had been on the far side of this a thousand times. He had never once been on the near side of it from a man who did it as he did it — no seal, no bracket, no tier; a ledger in a head and a sentence that said what the ledger said. He had no argument. The bench had been a cart.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-07.md:201:*Teague priced us. He's right about the cart. Oryn read me three times and got nothing three times and wants a fourth. Two people out here measuring me, and neither of them has a seal. I keep waiting to mind.*
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-01.md:9:*Inventory, because I do one at the start of anything. I am eighteen. Nine confirmed: Wind, Pressure, Iron, Compression, Ember, Storm, Anchor, Blade, and Shadow, which costs from waking whether I use it or not and drifted twice today on a road with nobody on it. Deployment since the Line: none. One anomaly. Session nine. Still open. Still real. Patience.*
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-01.md:151:Holding was never one act; it was a sequence of small refusals arriving faster than a person could name them. The Iron-adjacent read was the cheapest thing he owned and it wanted the ground under the thing's feet, wanted its weight and its gait through the surface of the valley, and he did not give it the ground. Anchor, which had been wanting fixed points in ordinary doorways since a road outside Ostrand, found the wash's near lip beside him and wanted a line laid along it, and he let it want. Shadow, which had been trying to switch itself on at dusk every evening since the Line, tried, and he held it off as he had held it off for three hours once in a room off a records hall, under the heaviest observation of his life. Wind sat where Wind sat. Storm sat where Storm sat, heavy. None of them moved.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-02.md:19:Then the slips themselves, which he had read in his first week for what they said and read now for what they were. Three kinds of paper. The holds' slips were good stock, cut square, in a steady hand, with a mark burned into one corner — a brand, an animal or a tool, the hold's own, because a hold had a fire and an iron and no seal. The brokers' slips were the thin paper brokers used everywhere, in a clerk's hand, the price written twice, once in words. And the carters' slips were in Pike's hand — every one — on the backs of older slips, the words short and spelled as they were said, because a carter came to the board and said what he wanted and Pike wrote it and read it back to him, and that was how a man who could not write posted a job.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-02.md:35:Cael read it the right way up. He had never read a page about himself the right way up. Every book he had ever been entered in had been turned away from him, or held, or kept in a drawer with a seal on the drawer, and he had learned to read upside down at fourteen for exactly that reason, and he stood at a plank on two barrels three days east of the Line and read four lines about five people with the writing running toward him, and let himself notice that it was easier, and filed the noticing.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-02.md:129:Cael had been fourteen at a table in Ardenmere with a woman on the far side of it and a bound book between them, and he had learned there, over one season, that a ledger could be the only honest record of a person — that the file with the seal on it could say *shattered* and the book with no seal could say what he had done, and one of them could be checked. He had carried that as a private doctrine through four institutions, and the institutions had marked him down for it, each in its own hand. And here it was, whole, run as a system at the scale of a country by a one-armed man on two barrels who priced holds' granaries by it and had never once needed to argue it, because out here it was the only arithmetic there was. He saw the shape of it entire and did not say so. He said the other thing.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-02.md:167:It was a carter's map. No seal, no registry hand, no dashes ending in nothing: the ford drawn as a line of dots across a brown river, Lowmarch as a square, the roads going out from it east and north and south in a plain firm line, the holds marked along them with their brands copied small, distances written in days. At the left-hand edge of the sheet, where a Compact map would have put the last verified waystation and stopped, the carter had drawn a single straight stroke, top to bottom, and written nothing beside it, because nobody out here needed to be told what it was.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-02.md:209:*Inventory, because I do one. Nine confirmed. One anomaly, session nine, still open, still real, patience. Deployment since the Line: none. Shadow tried again at dusk coming up from the landing, and I let it try, and it stopped. Anchor wants this doorway; it has wanted every doorway since the Ostrand road; I am letting it want. Eighteen. No injuries. Eighteen marks earned and about ten left after the jug.*
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-02.md:217:*No instrument out here reads me. No Arbiter station, no assessment panel, no registry seat with a seal on it. The board reads what we did. I've spent four years being the case the instruments couldn't parse, and it turns out the cure was a country with no instruments. Write it plainly, because it's true: I'm free. First time.*
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-04.md:171:Canvas, tarred, sealed at the neck with the Line's waystation mark in lead: mail, once a month, carried to the Line by the ferry and back with whatever had come the other way. The boy brought it up from the landing on his shoulder and set it on the plank's end, and Pike cut the lead with the pen-knife and turned the sack out and sorted, aloud, by name, because the board was where the names were.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-04.md:197:*Inventory. Nine confirmed, one anomaly, patience. Deployment since the Line: one, the ford, paid. Shadow tried at the landing when the ferry came in and stopped when I did nothing about it. Anchor wants the inn door. Eighteen. Calf: eleven paces on the flat, nine uphill, twelve down. Nobody mentions it, which I have decided to count as a kindness rather than a finding. Fifty-eight marks earned. Forty-eight in hand. No roof.*
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-09.md:9:The sack had come off the ferry at first light, tarred, lead-sealed at the neck with the waystation's mark, a month of the west in a bag, and he opened it with a knife and the one hand and read the names off the pieces aloud, at the same size as everything he read. Twenty-two pieces. The twenty-first and the twenty-second had Cael's name on them, and he knew both hands before the paper was in his.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-09.md:31:She walked on his left with her own sheet turned over: Pike's plank map, copied onto the back of the traverse in the last of the light on the ninetieth as far as the Fallow road's bend, and finished that morning while Pike wrote HOLD, because Karis did not copy part of a source. A carter's map. No seal, no brand, no waystation at either end of anything; the roads laid down by a hand that had walked them, and not straight, because the roads were not. On it the road under his feet ran solid from Lowmarch's ford to Thornwater's gate and past it, and every road on the sheet went to a name or a brand.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-09.md:65:Bracken had told the registry where they had gone. The Line, and nothing past it. He turned that over once and found it was the registry's sheet in his hand, said aloud by a registrar with his hands behind his back: seven dashes, ending in white. He filed it beside Withrow's tenure, which held, and the yard, which was quieter, and put the letter in the satchel against Vell's, still sealed.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-09.md:143:That was all of it. Five sentences and an initial. She had answered a letter he had sent west from a desk with no seal on it as she had answered every bout he had ever fought in front of her, which was by entering it. *Undefeated on paper.* He turned that twice, because she had chosen the last two words, and Vell chose nothing she had not weighed, and she had told him, in a strip of her own book, exactly where his line was kept and what it said and that in forty years there had not been another like it.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-03.md:115:He held the nine. The Iron-adjacent read wanted the river — the sill through his soles, the weight of whatever lay in the pools — and he did not give it the river. Shadow tried, because it was dusk. Anchor found the bar's line and wanted it. Wind sat where Wind sat, and he put a hand on it, privately, because if anyone spent it tonight it would be him, and told it to wait.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-03.md:253:*Seln did the shadow work tonight. I didn't. Out here there's a ledger and a ferry west once a month, and a defector's tradecraft in my hands would tell anyone who reads carefully where I learned it. The seal holds. Different country, same arithmetic.*
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-12.md:139:Karis's whole copy of Pike's plank, no seal, the roads solid, the Fallow road leaving Thornwater's east gate and running two days and bending, at the second day's rise, round a circle the carter had drawn as a circle, blank, and coming back to its line beyond it. Two other roads on the sheet did the same round two other blanks.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-12.md:175:Cael had it. It had ridden in the satchel against the leather book since the ninetieth day, thin broker's paper, the hook-and-bar seal in dark wax, and he unfolded it on the box beside the map. Seln did not pick it up. He read it where it lay, the whole of it, though it was four lines.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-12.md:223:*The contract, read again. Registry form under a broker's seal, for a kit that was never here, by someone who wanted to know which crew would go in. Seln: we just told them. The report goes west on the next ferry and it'll say we came out.*
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-11.md:115:Shadow-adjacent. Once.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-11.md:241:*Inventory. Nine confirmed, one anomaly. Deployment inside the line, all witnessed by the circle and by Oryn and by no one else on the continent: Shadow-adjacent, once, ten strides, to reach Karis. Then everything. Wind full, two bursts, two locks, a dog in the second. Iron the whole way. Pressure. Ember, one point, running. Blade. Storm. Compression at contact. Anchor, perceiving. All nine. First time I've spent the whole suite since Daeva. Bill: spent to the road; ribs at the tape; right calf open again on the lip, the ford's line; two landing beats.*
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-20.md:43:He had never held one. He had stood in front of a great many, and he stood in front of this one for the length of a breath before he let himself go to it, because it was a registry object in a place the registry had drawn as a hole, and it had the mark on it, and every registry object he had ever been near had been on the far side of a counter or a seal or a man's back. This one was on a floor, open, with its owner five years gone. It was theirs by the plain arithmetic of a contract, and it was the first thing with the registry's mark on it that had ever been *theirs*, and he catalogued that once and put it away, because the object was more interesting than the feeling.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-20.md:119:Nobody had discussed whether it would go up at all, because there had never been a question. The contract said *recovery*, and the kit was the instruments, and the crew had walked into two floors for it and had a man shot on the second, and the question of what a crew does with a thing it was hired to recover, when the man who hired it was the man who had built the trap on the rim, had been answered at a plank at Lowmarch by a defector with a finger on a slip, and had not needed to be asked again in a chamber.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-18.md:49:He read it. All of it, the site and the price and the seal, and then Seln's anatomy of it, in Seln's order, shorter, because Seln was standing at the lean-to's corner and did not need to hear his own report twice, and then the council's four positions and the decision, and the day south, and the two days back, and a crew coming to look if they were late. She listened without a verdict, going along it.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-19.md:99:He had held it through a country with a ledger and a ferry west, and used it once on a floor with the circle for witnesses and nobody else, and the seal on it was the seal it had always been: a defector's tradecraft in his hands, shown to anyone who could carry the seeing west, would burn the man at the doorway. The rim could not carry what it never perceived, and the fragment's whole function was that it was not perceived. He had never once had a use for it that was not also the reason it could not be used. He had one now.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-19.md:123:Shadow-adjacent. He let it off its hold at the tenth step, where the square of grey was still small, and felt it come up the way it always came up: the presence going out of him like a lamp turned down, the movement folded into whatever the light was doing, the thing that made a man a place where nobody's eye stopped. It ran inside the line exactly as it ran outside it, as everything he owned had run on a floor two days east, and he catalogued that once more, going up, as a fact and not a reason.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-19.md:171:He catalogued himself last, sitting in the rut with the stone at his back. Spent. The suite gone out of him on the rim as it had gone out on a floor against a Gold, on top of a week's rest that was not rest enough for it, the bill entered at the usual rate plus the rim's; the calf and the ribs saying nothing, which was new; nineteen. Shadow-adjacent used once, inside, in front of nobody who could carry it anywhere, and the seal the seal it had always been. He had not read anyone. He had asked nobody, because he had not needed to, and he had said so on a floor to a healer who had wanted it said.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-19.md:175:He came back down it with a crossbow in his hand — one of the four — and stood on the road in the lantern's edge and looked at it, and the five of them looked at him, because Seln with a thing in his hand that he had not put there himself was a thing none of them had seen. He turned it over. Road-made. No mark on it. No seal, no maker's stamp, no number, nothing on it that a man could carry to a plank and say *this*.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-14.md:175:He caught himself, somewhere past the middle of it, running the room as an instrument — six places, one board, two doors, a fire, the light in the window gone from yellow to nothing — and put the instrument down. Not the new one. He did not have a hand on that yet. The old one; the count. He sat in a common room in a walled steading two days east of Lowmarch, with five people who knew both his ledgers, on a birthday nobody with a seal knew the date of, and for the better part of an hour did nothing with any of it, which was the second time this year and the first time on ground no registry had drawn.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-14.md:205:*Inventory. Ten confirmed. Wind-adjacent — Lira. Pressure-adjacent — the Bronze from Ardenmere. Iron-adjacent — Brom. Compression-adjacent — Reydan. Ember-adjacent — Karis. Shadow-adjacent — source protected; the seal holds out here as it held inside; used once past the Line, on a floor, in front of the circle and nobody else. Storm-adjacent — Daeva; resettled once this week round the new neighbour, on the second morning, in the yard, and I heard it do it. Anchor-adjacent — unnamed; the hand with no face; it wanted the stake and it can want. Blade-adjacent — Ephram; the only non-hostile in the log until three days ago. Tide-adjacent — Oryn. Partial. Iron. Self and contact. Engagement: clinical. Does nothing in a fight, and is the first thing I've ever owned that lets me hear the other nine. Cost: three days of listening; the idle state learned by the third night, and it isn't quiet, it's just that I've stopped putting my hand to every string. Deployment notes current on all ten.*
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-13.md:173:Shadow. He felt her go along it, and felt it draw in as it always drew in, and felt her feel that — a movement that got quieter as she touched it — and go on, and say nothing, because she had said she would say what she felt and not what it meant, and a movement that hid was a meaning.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-13.md:215:A count coming out even: a column he had not known he was adding arriving at its foot, and the foot being a number, and the number being *her* — how the current went where it was put; how it lay along a thing to learn it; how it went and did not push; how it kept time. The reaching was the interior action it had always been, on a yard's floor and in a sealed room and at a road cut and at a flag: attention turned wholly on a witnessed architecture and held there, open, without demand. It was running now on a plank table in a steading, with the witnessed architecture inside him, still, its hands on him, and it was going to complete.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-15.md:57:"Five sigils." Her hand went to five points on the table, one for each. "Wind. Iron Skin. Ember. Shadow. Tide. Iron rank one, Copper, no ranking, Bronze, Iron rank four. Five people, five Paths, five standings, five histories of a thing that showed up at fourteen and has been each of ours since." She looked at the five points. "Every one of them went out at the same stride. Not the same stretch of road — the same *stride*. Lira called it and I called it and I never once had to correct her by a foot. Every one of them went out the same way: not dimming, not failing, not going wrong first — there, and then not there. And every one came back the same: at the stride, in place, the clock started again without having lost the time. Lira's words. Oryn's. Brom's, if Brom used words."
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-15.md:83:She cleared the table of everything but the chips, and laid them out in a row on their cloths, oldest to the right, and named each one as she set it down, the way she had named the stakes on the mountain and the documents in a sealed room, so that the room would have the ruler before it had the measurement.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-15.md:119:Cael put the slip back in the satchel, flat, against the leather book, where it had ridden since the ninetieth day. It was four lines of thin paper under a hook-and-bar seal, and it had walked six people two days east and one of them over a line eight times alone, and it had cost an arm a season and a throat a week, and it had been written by somebody who had known there was nothing at the site to recover and had wanted to know who would go and look. He did not write that. Karis had entered the stakes once, and once was the number, and he had learned from a man at a stone that a thing said once had a number and that the number was its weight.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-15.md:131:"Observation. Inside the perimeter, at every trial and in the fight, five practitioners' Paths did not render: Wind, Iron Skin, Ember, Shadow, Tide. Inside the perimeter, at two trials and in the fight, the sixth practitioner's — " she stopped, and started the word again, and it came out level — "the sixth practitioner's *fragments* rendered. All of them. Iron read, the whole day. Wind evasion, a foot's worth, silent, at twenty meters in. In the fight: Shadow, Wind full, Pressure, Ember, Blade, Storm, Compression at contact, Anchor perceiving. Nine of nine. Witnessed by five. His Arbiter unchanged either side of the line, stated by him." She wrote the last word and lifted the pen. "That's the observation, and it's the largest thing on this table, and I have it, and I'm going to enter it exactly like that and not one word further."
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-17.md:47:He saw the seal before he saw the paper. Dark wax, a hook and a bar, in the corner — the landing broker's, the one on a third of the bales — and thin broker's stock under it, and a clerk's hand, even, schooled, the letters sitting on a line that had not been drawn. He read it from the doorway, upside down, and then took it off its nail and read it the right way up at the plank, and Karis read it over his shoulder, and Seln did not read it at all, because Seln had read it from the doorway with the rest of the wall and had already gone still in the way he went still at a shelf.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-17.md:51:Then the seal, and nothing else. No crew's mark. No HOLD. No name beside a word.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-17.md:71:"Nothing else posted in a year. A broker doesn't post recovery contracts; he moves bales. He's posted two, both for instruments that were never at the site, both in prose he didn't write, both under his own seal, and the second the day after the first's report went west." The finger moved to the price. "Three hundred. Read it as a number and it's an incentive. Read it as a size and it's something else. A hundred was priced so that no crew who knew the road would take it, and one crew who didn't would. Three hundred is priced for *one* crew. The crew that went in once and came out. Nobody else on this wall will touch it at any price, and he knows that, because the report told him, and he's put a number on it that a crew of six with a mule and a hundred and two in hand can't walk past. That's not a wage. That's a lure, cut to fit."
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-22.md:95:It was not the board's question. The board asked what you had *done*. This was the other question, the one every instrument on the continent had asked and had not been able to answer and had filed instead, and here it was, on a marsh, from a man with no seal, asked plainly, like a man who actually wanted to know the answer.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-24.md:67:*Wind-adjacent — Lira. Pressure-adjacent — the Bronze from Ardenmere. Iron-adjacent — Brom. Compression-adjacent — Reydan. Ember-adjacent — Karis. Shadow-adjacent — Seln; source protected; the seal held past the Line as it held inside, and was used twice out here where no one who could carry it west could see it, and that is the seal's whole logic and it is unchanged. Storm-adjacent — Daeva. Anchor-adjacent — unnamed; the hand with no face; opened a door on a rim and held a floor in a hall, and I own a piece of the craft the system uses to hold people and have used it twice this year, once each way. Blade-adjacent — Ephram. Tide-adjacent — Oryn; partial; Iron; self and contact; the tenth, and the first that does nothing in a fight, and the only one whose engagement field says clinical, and the only one with a rule on it in her hand and mine: ask first, every time. Deployment notes current on all ten.*
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-24.md:99:He sealed both, and put them in the tarred sack himself, at the landing, in the snow, and watched the ferryman put the Line's lead on it.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-24.md:129:Nobody answered. Nobody said who, or why, or how old. Lira looked at the line. Brom looked at the straightedges, both of them, brass on brass. Seln looked at the map's edge where the line went off it. Cael looked at the fourth circle, twice the size of the others, with a cordon round it that nobody had drawn on the sheet because nobody had needed to. The lamp's light lay along the brass and the brass laid its shadow along the line, a hair's width, the length of the map, and the five of them looked at the shadow for four seconds, by his count, and then Karis put her hand flat on the sheet, and the evening closed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/BOOK_MAP.md:20:- **The seal:** Shadow-adjacent is sealed from any witness who could carry it west, because a defector's tradecraft in Cael's hands would confirm the surveillance-era acquisition. It costs from waking and drifts at dusk. The seal holds out here.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/BOOK_MAP.md:21:- **Companions:** Lira, Wind, Iron R1 formal (Silver bracket declined at full price). Brom, Iron Skin, Copper formal for good, continental Copper champion. Karis, Ember, ranking forfeited, three boxes made into a field archive. Seln, Shadow, Bronze, left the Compact in form, carrying the locked case (the machine's own maintenance records). All uninjured. Lira and Brom have read the log. Karis sees demonstrations, not the log. Seln knows the mechanism since B6 Ch15.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/BOOK_MAP.md:64:| 9 | Binding in the barn; four of five; "none of you shouted"; the seal log; "First honest number" (Ch3) | 53–57 | KEEP |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/BOOK_MAP.md:78:| 23 | **The Ring at dusk:** nine hounds; the cut road and the cord-lane; Brom at the lip; Lira's arm; Karis down; Shadow once; the full suite; the pack led out; Karis's wall at the line (Ch11) | 96 | KEEP |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/BOOK_MAP.md:88:| 33 | **The trap on the Long Stair:** the bolt; the Anchor course; the stair and the chamber; the stalemate; Shadow up the stair; the course unbound from inside; out of the Quiet declaring; the rim breaks; no faces; Teague: "You're something the board doesn't have a column for." (Ch19) | 114 | KEEP |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/BOOK_MAP.md:102:1. **The brief cutaway system (+40,300, all new).** Five named companions-or-rivals at about 2.5–3% each, plus Vastin's single three-sentence window (§5). *Why:* the formula's ~13% secondary share. This book is also about *being priced*, so each window shows one of the people doing the pricing: the healer, the rival captain, the defector, the researcher, the partner.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/BOOK_MAP.md:112:**Do not add:** any new fight with stillhounds before the Ring, which would spend the Ring's surprise. Any Compact face, name, policy, directive or office scene beyond Vastin's window. Any new Shadow-adjacent use (it is used exactly twice: Ring, Stair). Any fragment use in M1–M3 beyond the ford's single burst (the log counts "Deployment since the Line: one" through day 90). Any Daeva or Reydan contact beyond one log line each. Any Ephram, Withrow, Rooke or Bracken scene (letters only). Any Coss, Havel or Ilsev. Any Teague-crew name.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/BOOK_MAP.md:180:**How the first third teaches hard without spending anything:** the log's inventory of nine, with each fragment's idle rent named (Shadow at dusk, Anchor wanting doorways, Storm heavy); the discipline of holding as a skill with failure modes; the board's arithmetic (outcome, not tier); the carters' "don't declare"; the ford's discharge finding; the shale-backs' flight; Oryn's Tide taught in full (contact only, the reserve, three mendings a day, cannot heal herself, "cannot mend what I can't read," two readings); tier talk at the plank (Pike's Copper Force, Teague's unreported Bronze, Lira's Iron R1, Brom's Copper forever). **Cael deploys nothing in M1–M3 except the single ford burst.** The log reads "Deployment since the Line: one" from day 53 to day 90, and the edition keeps that count.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/BOOK_MAP.md:184:| **The hold** (method; not a fragment) | M1 taught as a discipline and given failure modes (Shadow drifting at dusk, Anchor's wants); M2 inverted at the ford (bait with one, kill with everything that isn't one); M5 dropped all at once inside the Ring | A sequence of small refusals, "arriving faster than a person could name them" |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/BOOK_MAP.md:188:| **Shadow-adjacent** (sealed) | Drifts at dusk in M1–M4 and is held. **Used exactly twice:** M5 ten strides to reach Karis (circle and Oryn only); M7 up the stair and across the bowl, unseen by the rim, never attributable | Costs from waking; the seal's whole logic is restated once (M2 log) and honored |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/BOOK_MAP.md:246:- The Fallow slip (Ch8, Ch12): *Recovery. Surveyor's instruments abandoned at a site on the Fallow road. East of Thornwater; the second day; where the road leaves its line at the rise and comes back to it. One hundred marks (one hundred).* Seal: a hook and a bar, in dark wax.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/BOOK_MAP.md:264:- Ephram (Ch9), whole letter: *Cael — / Weather first, because you'll want it in order, and I've watched you want things in order. / The Crown yard has a new cohort captain. The intake came up the bluff a fortnight ago, forty-one of them, and they've put a third-year over them who was a second-year when you left and whose name you would not know, so I'll not spend the ink. He is fair. He counts slower than you did. They stand where he puts them. / Withrow's tenure holds. That is the whole of the sentence. I asked whether there was a longer one and was told there was not, in a way that meant there had been, for a week. / A query came down to the registry from somewhere above it. Bracken would not say where and I did not ask twice. It wanted former enrollees' last known direction of travel. Bracken answered it. He gave them a map reference to the Line and nothing past it — which is true, and which Bracken enjoyed being true. He told me so at the plaque wall with his hands behind his back, and then told me he had not told me. / I still lose to you every time I run it. I have stopped running it in the yard. / Rooke says the yard's quieter. He didn't say it was worse. He also didn't say it wasn't. / — E.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/BOOK_MAP.md:273:- Ch2, the first pole: *No instrument out here reads me. No Arbiter station, no assessment panel, no registry seat with a seal on it. The board reads what we did. I've spent four years being the case the instruments couldn't parse, and it turns out the cure was a country with no instruments. Write it plainly, because it's true: I'm free. First time.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/BOOK_MAP.md:274:- Ch3, the seal: *Seln did the shadow work tonight. I didn't. Out here there's a ledger and a ferry west once a month, and a defector's tradecraft in my hands would tell anyone who reads carefully where I learned it. The seal holds. Different country, same arithmetic.* — and *First honest number anyone's written about us in a year. It says four of five got hurt. It's right.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/BOOK_MAP.md:277:- Ch7: *Teague priced us. He's right about the cart. Oryn read me three times and got nothing three times and wants a fourth. Two people out here measuring me, and neither of them has a seal. I keep waiting to mind.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/BOOK_MAP.md:295:- Ch24, the inventory: *Ten confirmed fragments.* / *Wind-adjacent — Lira. Pressure-adjacent — the Bronze from Ardenmere. Iron-adjacent — Brom. Compression-adjacent — Reydan. Ember-adjacent — Karis. Shadow-adjacent — Seln; source protected; the seal held past the Line as it held inside, and was used twice out here where no one who could carry it west could see it, and that is the seal's whole logic and it is unchanged. Storm-adjacent — Daeva. Anchor-adjacent — unnamed; the hand with no face; opened a door on a rim and held a floor in a hall, and I own a piece of the craft the system uses to hold people and have used it twice this year, once each way. Blade-adjacent — Ephram. Tide-adjacent — Oryn; partial; Iron; self and contact; the tenth, and the first that does nothing in a fight, and the only one whose engagement field says clinical, and the only one with a rule on it in her hand and mine: ask first, every time. Deployment notes current on all ten.* / *One anomaly. Session nine. Tide-adjacent. Not an entry any more; a location. The still place. Read three times. Doesn't flow. Doesn't change. Everything goes around it. Not the Quiet. Not a fragment. Older than the log. Observation continues.* / *Reydan will get his answer. Daeva will get her rematch. Both are going to need a map.* — **Audio rule:** Feryn's name never appears in a list run beside Oryn's; every inventory says "the Bronze from Ardenmere."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/BOOK_MAP.md:329:| Integration mechanism (revealed B3; sealed to the circle) | Oryn learns it on the 100th, told in full, the covenant page read; the circle becomes six | Tell Teague, Pike, any hold, the rim; let any cutaway outside the six guess it |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/BOOK_MAP.md:340:| The faceless faction | Two reaches: paper (Fallow) and in person (Stair); six hooded, road grey, crossbows, a second Anchor hand | Faces, names, seals, an office, a location; any knowledge that fragments work *inside* (the rim saw only the declaration outside) |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/BOOK_MAP.md:350:- **C1 · The cart, the horse and the purse vanish across the seam.** B6 Ch24 ends with "A cart. Three boxes and a case under one canvas. A horse." Karis has read out a purse "more than any of them had expected." B7 never mentions the cart or horse. The boxes ride a carter's grain (Ch1); the crew hires the smith's cart (Ch5); by day 49 they have "Eighteen marks earned and about ten left after the jug" and sleep in the meadow. **Recommend (default):** stage it in M1. The horse is a city animal on mule roads (fodder, noise, "paper" written on it), and the cart is too wide for the scrub ruts past Lowmarch. They are sold in Lowmarch's first week. The sale and what remains of the Book 6 purse become a sealed **reserve** that the crew agrees not to live on (Brom's rule: we eat what the board pays). The log's "in hand" counts only the board purse, so every figure in the prose and in Book 8 stands. *Alternative:* the purse was spent on the road and the sale bought gear; the author must then account for the sale money by day 49. **Owner call.**
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/packets/MOVEMENT-002.md:10:- Formula load: progression vocabulary ≈82 per 10k (discharge, declaration, Iron Skin as a shout, the lattice point, the seal restated). ≈15 growth beats. Supporting beats for Lira, Brom, Karis, Seln, Pike, the steading's woman, the bowman.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/packets/MOVEMENT-002.md:26:- **Costs on the page:** Brom's forearm open to the bone, wrist to mid-arm; Lira's right ankle, bound flat; Karis's palms cut to the meat, unable to hold a pen; Cael's calf bitten through, in and out, felt only when the water went cold. "Four… Out of five." The bowman: "none of you shouted." **The seal log** (protected; the only restatement). Pike's line by carter; "First honest number…"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/packets/MOVEMENT-002.md:34:- **Deployment since the Line: one** (the ford burst), from day 53 onward. No other fragment use, private or public. **Shadow unused.**
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/packets/MOVEMENT-007.md:12:**Read before drafting:** B7 source `chapter-17.md`, `chapter-18.md`, `chapter-19.md`, `chapter-20.md`; `BOOK_MAP.md` §7 (Shadow twice; Anchor unbinding), §8 (Stair allowance), §10 (Ch17–20), §11 (faceless; the rim sees only the outside), §12 C3; `STATE_LEDGER.md` (Movement 6 append); `v3-runs/book-07/CONFLICT-B7-CH18.md` (why Millrace).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/packets/MOVEMENT-007.md:29:- **Day 114, the trap (≈9,000–10,500 across two chapters).** The crossing in Seln's order. **The bolt at forty meters**: "Ridge." Six hooded on the rim in road grey (two south, two east, one west, one north). **The Anchor course** closing the line behind them; Cael sees it with perception only. Bolts skip off the fused plate. **Lira runs *down***, and the unit follows. **Forty steps.** The chamber, ten strides square, its ceiling the floor; walls carved; at the middle "something cased… a cord," unread. **The doorway**: "They can shoot the bowl… They can't shoot the stair." **Oryn without hands**: lamp, eyes, two fingers; "Tape it. Leave the bolt."; "the bolt's the plug"; "I came south for exactly this. Enter that." Lira's mend reopened on the stair wall. **The stalemate, given room**: the square of grey going white and grey; a bolt sent down to stop at the thirty-first step; Brom grey and not worse; Karis with her back to the wall she would give a year to read. The design read aloud ("It thinks the floor did the same thing to all six of us."). **The order:** up alone with the one he was given. Oryn: "Ask first." / "I'm not reading anyone." / "I know. I'm saying the rule out loud so it's been said on this floor too." Lira: "Later." **The ascent, Shadow alone**, across the bowl unseen; the rim scanning for a shout that cannot exist. Anchor let off at ten meters as perception, then as a hand: **the knot found and released**, the course going slack like rigging. **Over the stone, declaring**: Wind, Storm seeding the rut, Compression at contact, Ember held out. He removes one man from the plan and does not fight him. The rim breaks as professionals break. The west one looks back for a second, hood up, nothing in it. Four crossbows left; no faces. "Back."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/packets/MOVEMENT-007.md:35:- **Shadow is used once in this movement**, unseen and never attributable. That is its second and last use in the book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/packets/MOVEMENT-007.md:37:- Faceless: no seals, no names, no office. The specialist is not the Ostrand-road woman: the same craft, a different weight in the laying.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/packets/MOVEMENT-003.md:48:Day 84: a hundred and sixteen in hand, six owed to the smith for a cart in a gorge. Three ribs taped, a calf bound in her cloth, a leg that is new, an ankle taped, a wrist mended and not to pull. Three failed readings, and a healer who wants a fourth "when you're not broken." Thornwater in nine days. Two people out here are measuring him, and neither has a seal.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/packets/MOVEMENT-004.md:23:- **Lowmarch, day 90:** two hold slips with HOLD and **five names** ("Holds came to the ferry. Asked who. I said you."), waiting a fortnight or Teague. The traverse line read and priced: "Sixty-four's what the job was worth." / "Fair." **The posting under the stone's far edge**, the dust-curve on it, the hook-and-bar seal in wax; he reads it upside down, then gives it to Pike. Pike's face stops; Cael counts nine. "That's the Fallow Ring." / "That's quiet ground." **Pike's speech** (protected) and "You'll know. Your Arbiter tells you. It stops." Karis brings the page with two lines under it from the middle box: "A location. And a contract." The smith paid: a hundred and eight. Lira at the roof's edge: "Two hundred times. He counted." / "So do you. I've never once heard you say never."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/packets/MOVEMENT-004.md:34:- Only the two private tests, unwitnessed, never logged as deployment. Shadow untouched. Nobody sees him do anything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/packets/MOVEMENT-001.md:24:- **C1 (default unless the owner rules otherwise):** in Lowmarch's first week the horse and cart are sold. A horse is fodder and a sign that says *paper*; the scrub ruts past Lowmarch take mules. The sale and what is left of the Book 6 purse become a sealed reserve that Brom's rule keeps them from living on. The log's "in hand" counts only what the board pays. Give it one good scene and an argument with a joke in it, then let it go.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/packets/MOVEMENT-001.md:27:- **The hold as a learning arc.** Lira trying runs that end with nothing (a burst is a shout); Brom carrying a load and not hardening; Karis keeping one point she will not light; Cael cataloguing the nine as things *held*, each with its rent: Shadow drifting at dusk (twice, on a road with nobody on it), Anchor wanting every doorway since the Ostrand road, Storm sitting heavy. **No fragment is used in this movement.** The log reads "Deployment since the Line: none" through day 49.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/packets/MOVEMENT-005.md:22:- **The Ring (≈8,000–9,500 across two chapters), a retreat with a geometry.** The line is the objective. Nine counted on the white. "The road's cut." / "The grass. Where the cord went." Brom holds the lip: a hand's-height drop that robs a hound of drive, the haft, a man the pack cannot hear. Lira runs the rim's turned edge; the ankle goes a third time; the forearm taken and held; she cannot burst it off. **Brom's bolt through the eye at four meters.** Oryn's hands: "Nothing… Nothing in my hands." Cloth and knees. "Move. I can't fix this here. Move." Brom's string opens his fingers in the dark. The lane counted down, 160 to 60, the scrub moving at Lira's pace and never showing a shape. They are *careless* in here, because no prey has ever shouted. **Karis down at sixty**, hands on a throat, the jaws closing across her fists, the loudest sound on the ring. **Shadow, once, ten strides** (circle and Oryn only). The throw. Then *he stops holding*: Wind full, north off the lane; every head turns; then everything, one after another, so there is never a breath when he is not the loudest thing on the floor. Pressure for noise; Ember held out; Blade ringing with nothing to cut; Storm seeding the rut; Anchor off its hold and looking west. North to the rut, **east the wrong way** about a hundred and twenty meters, back west. Seln's "Back" from the stone and Lira's a breath after. The second lock with Compression at contact. Over the stone. **The bolt at two meters.** Anchor's perception sees Karis's points before they light: **the wall of ignition along the line's inside edge**, from her knees, voiceless. Seven break out north and south along the outside; two dead. Win-sentence and costs on the page. The opponent learned nothing it could use, because it had never needed to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/packets/MOVEMENT-005.md:27:- **Day 100, the deep reading (≈4,500):** the back room fixed from the door (window, table, stool, the roll in her order); "Breathe like a man asleep."; the count of ten ("Nothing. As before."); then the long one. The current goes *along*, not down; she can be felt keeping time; her commentary on banks (Iron Skin walled, Ember hot, Wind too fast to feel), "there's more water"; Shadow draws in under her touch; Storm quiet; he understands she is not afraid of him, because her trade has no room for being impressed; "Eleven years…". **The conditions arrive.** He could stop it, and stopping would be the lie. *He reached. Directed. Open-eyed. Still.* **The notice, exactly.** Two firsts counted; the third not written. "Something just changed…" / "No. I did. I need to tell you what I am." / "Then lie still while you do it…"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/packets/MOVEMENT-005.md:31:- Five witnesses to the Ring and no one else. The pack is not a witness. Shadow is used **once**.

